// CH1_EXTRA
const SOCIAL_CH1_EXTRA = [
  { q: "Frederic Sorrieu's painting 'The Dream of Worldwide Democratic and Social Republics' (1848) depicted nations marching in a specific order. Which nation was placed at the FRONT of the procession?", opts: ['Germany', 'United States of America', 'France', 'Britain'], ans: 2, exp: "In Sorrieu's utopian painting, France leads the procession of nations toward the statue of Liberty, reflecting France's self-image as the birthplace of Revolution and liberty. The painting was created in 1848, the year of widespread European revolutions, and expressed the liberal-nationalist dream of a fraternal world order." },
  { q: "In Sorrieu's 1848 utopian print, the peoples of the world are shown marching past which symbolic structure?", opts: ['The Palace of Versailles', 'A statue of Christ and angels bearing the torch of Enlightenment', 'The Frankfurt Parliament building', 'The Arc de Triomphe'], ans: 1, exp: "Sorrieu's painting shows the peoples of the world marching past a colossal statue of Christ, with angels in the sky bearing the torch of Enlightenment — symbolising the divine blessing of freedom and the universality of nationalist-democratic ideals. This spiritual imagery was intended to legitimise the concept of free nation-states." },
  { q: "Eugène Delacroix's painting 'Liberty Leading the People' (1830) commemorated which specific event?", opts: ['The French Revolution of 1789', 'The July Revolution of 1830 in France', 'The Revolution of 1848 in France', 'Napoleon\'s coronation in 1804'], ans: 1, exp: "Delacroix painted 'Liberty Leading the People' to commemorate the July Revolution of 1830, when Parisians overthrew King Charles X. The allegorical female figure of Liberty, bearing the tricolour flag and stepping over bodies, embodied both the Republican ideal and the power of popular uprising." },
  { q: "In the allegorical paintings of Germania used during German nationalist movements, the oak crown Germania wears symbolised:", opts: ['Military victory over Napoleon', 'The ancient and enduring strength of the German people', 'The Holy Roman Empire\'s legacy', 'Economic prosperity from the Zollverein'], ans: 1, exp: "In German allegory, oak leaves and the oak crown symbolised the ancient, deep-rooted strength and heroism of the German people — the oak being a sacred tree in Germanic mythology. Paintings of Germania often showed her wearing an oak crown alongside a sword and olive branch to combine martial and peaceful ideals." },
  { q: "The Germania allegory painted by Philipp Veit for the Frankfurt Parliament (1848) depicted her with broken chains at her wrists. What did this specific detail symbolise?", opts: ['Germany\'s defeat of Austria in the Austro-Prussian War', 'The overthrow of Napoleonic occupation', 'Freedom from princely despotism and the dawn of liberal constitutional nationalism', 'The end of serfdom in German states'], ans: 2, exp: "The broken chains on Frankfurt Parliament's Germania represented liberation from the tyrannical rule of German princes and the conservative order imposed by Metternich. This image was painted during the liberal revolution of 1848, when German nationalists hoped to create a unified constitutional Germany." },
  { q: "The Carlsbad Decrees of 1819 were issued under the influence of Metternich primarily to suppress:", opts: ['The Polish nationalist uprising in Warsaw', 'Liberal and nationalist student movements (Burschenschaften) in German universities', 'The Italian Carbonari secret societies', 'The Silesian weavers\' revolt'], ans: 1, exp: "The Carlsbad Decrees (1819) were Metternich's response to the murder of conservative playwright Kotzebue by a nationalist student. They dissolved the Burschenschaften (student nationalist societies), imposed censorship on universities and the press, and established a surveillance system — effectively crushing liberal nationalism in German states for decades." },
  { q: "The Burschenschaften were German university student organisations that became targets of repression because they promoted:", opts: ['Socialist economic reforms and redistribution of land', 'Pan-European empire under Austrian leadership', 'Nationalist ideas of a unified German state with liberal constitutions', 'Restoration of the Holy Roman Empire under Habsburg rule'], ans: 2, exp: "The Burschenschaften (fraternal societies, founded 1815 at Jena) promoted German nationalism and liberal constitutionalism. Their Wartburg Festival (1817) celebrated Luther and the Battle of Leipzig, burning reactionary books. Metternich used the assassination of Kotzebue (1819) to justify the Carlsbad Decrees dissolving these societies." },
  { q: "The Frankfurt Parliament of 1848 ultimately failed because King Frederick William IV of Prussia took which position?", opts: ['He accepted the crown but imposed authoritarian conditions', 'He agreed to unify Germany but insisted Austria be included', 'He refused the crown offered by an elected assembly, calling it a \'crown from the gutter\'', 'He was deposed before he could respond'], ans: 2, exp: "Frederick William IV contemptuously rejected the German crown offered by the Frankfurt Parliament in April 1849, saying he would not 'pick up a crown from the gutter' — meaning he refused a crown granted by a popularly elected liberal assembly rather than by the princes. This doomed the Parliament, which lacked military force to compel him." },
  { q: "Beyond Frederick William IV's refusal, which of the following was ALSO a key structural reason for the Frankfurt Parliament's failure?", opts: ['The Parliament met in only one session before dissolving', 'The Parliament debated constitutions for 10 years without voting', 'The Parliament had no army of its own and depended on the goodwill of princes who ultimately withdrew support', 'France militarily invaded to prevent German unification'], ans: 2, exp: "The Frankfurt Parliament lacked coercive power — it had no army and depended on the Prussian and Austrian armies, which ultimately crushed the revolution. Additionally, the middle-class liberals who dominated it feared the rising demands of workers and peasants, causing the coalition to fragment. Conservative forces exploited this weakness." },
  { q: "The Zollverein of 1834 initially excluded which major German-speaking power, and why was this significant for future unification?", opts: ['Prussia, because it had its own separate tariff system', 'Austria, which weakened its influence over German economic affairs and strengthened Prussia\'s leadership', 'Bavaria, which preferred French economic ties', 'Saxony, which had industrial protectionist policies'], ans: 1, exp: "The Zollverein excluded Austria, significantly weakening Vienna's economic grip over German states and positioning Prussia as the natural economic — and later political — leader of Germany. By 1844, the Zollverein had 39 of 39 German states except Austria, prefiguring the 'Lesser Germany' (Kleindeutschland) solution of unification without Austria." },
  { q: "Which of the following BEST distinguishes Giuseppe Mazzini's approach to Italian unification from Count Cavour's?", opts: ['Mazzini wanted unification through royal diplomacy; Cavour through revolution', 'Mazzini believed in a democratic republic through popular uprising; Cavour used diplomatic alliances and monarchical power from above', 'Mazzini focused only on northern Italy; Cavour on the south', 'Mazzini supported Austrian help; Cavour opposed it'], ans: 1, exp: "Mazzini was a republican who believed Italian unification must come through a popular democratic revolution from below, inspired by his Young Italy movement. Cavour, as Piedmont-Sardinia's Prime Minister, used Realpolitik — alliances with Napoleon III of France and diplomatic manoeuvring to engineer unification under the Savoy monarchy." },
  { q: "How did Garibaldi's approach to Italian unification differ from both Mazzini AND Cavour?", opts: ['Garibaldi relied on foreign military alliances like Cavour but for a republic like Mazzini', 'Garibaldi used direct military action leading volunteer guerrilla forces, operating independently of diplomatic strategy but ultimately handed conquests to the monarchy', 'Garibaldi opposed unification and preferred southern Italian independence', 'Garibaldi led the Piedmontese regular army unlike Mazzini and Cavour'], ans: 1, exp: "Garibaldi's approach was direct military action with volunteer forces (the Red Shirts) — he conquered the Kingdom of the Two Sicilies in 1860 through guerrilla warfare. Unlike Mazzini's intellectual republicanism or Cavour's diplomatic chess, Garibaldi acted on the ground. Despite being a republican, he pragmatically handed his conquests to King Victor Emmanuel II to achieve unification." },
  { q: "The Treaty of Vienna 1815 established the principle of 'legitimacy'. What did this specifically mean in practice?", opts: ['Nations had the legitimate right to self-determination', 'The Congress would legitimise any government that controlled a territory effectively', 'Monarchies overthrown by Napoleon were to be restored to their thrones as the legitimate rulers', 'Austria was legitimately recognised as the dominant power in Europe'], ans: 2, exp: "The principle of legitimacy meant that 'legitimate' monarchs — those ruling before Napoleon — would be restored to their thrones. The Bourbons were restored in France, Spain, and Naples. This conservative principle directly opposed nationalist self-determination and aimed to return Europe to pre-1789 social-political arrangements." },
  { q: "The Concert of Europe, established after 1815, was designed to:", opts: ['Create a European parliament with representatives from all nations', 'Promote free trade between European nations to prevent economic conflicts', 'Coordinate European great powers to suppress revolutionary and nationalist uprisings and maintain the balance of power', 'Develop a common European military force against Russian expansion'], ans: 2, exp: "The Concert of Europe was the system of great-power congresses (Aix-la-Chapelle 1818, Troppau 1820, Laibach 1821, Verona 1822) through which Austria, Prussia, Russia, and Britain cooperated to suppress revolutions and maintain the conservative order established at Vienna. It was Metternich's primary tool for maintaining stability through collective intervention." },
  { q: "Metternich described nationalism as 'a disease' and conservatives viewed it as a threat because:", opts: ['Nationalism would lead to democratic participation which threatened the aristocratic social order and the multi-ethnic Habsburg Empire specifically', 'Nationalism promoted industrialisation which would make the aristocracy economically obsolete', 'Nationalism was associated with atheism and threatened the Church-State alliance', 'Nationalists wanted to restore the Holy Roman Empire which Metternich had helped dissolve'], ans: 0, exp: "Metternich was Austria's chief minister, ruling a multi-ethnic empire (Germans, Hungarians, Czechs, Italians, Poles, Croats, etc.). Nationalism threatened this empire existentially — each group might demand its own state. Additionally, nationalism was intertwined with liberalism and democracy, threatening aristocratic privilege and absolute monarchy throughout Europe." },
  { q: "The Kalevala, compiled by Elias Lönnrot in 1835, was significant for Finnish nationalism because:", opts: ['It was Finland\'s first constitution demanding independence from Russia', 'It was a compilation of Finnish oral folk poetry that created a sense of shared cultural identity and historical roots separate from Swedish and Russian influences', 'It established Finnish as the official language of the Finnish Diet', 'It described Finnish military victories over Sweden'], ans: 1, exp: "The Kalevala (1835, expanded 1849) compiled Finnish oral tradition, mythology, and epic poetry collected by Elias Lönnrot. It gave Finns a sense of ancient cultural identity distinct from their Swedish-speaking elite and Russian imperial rulers, becoming a cornerstone of Finnish national consciousness and the movement for Finnish language rights and autonomy." },
  { q: "Otto von Bismarck's war against Denmark in 1864 was specifically fought over:", opts: ['Control of the port of Hamburg and North Sea trade routes', 'The duchies of Schleswig and Holstein, which had mixed German and Danish populations', 'Denmark\'s refusal to join the Zollverein customs union', 'Disputed fishing rights in the Baltic Sea'], ans: 1, exp: "Prussia and Austria jointly fought Denmark in 1864 over the duchies of Schleswig (mixed German-Danish) and Holstein (majority German). Denmark was defeated and ceded the duchies. Bismarck then used the dispute over administering these territories as a pretext for the Austro-Prussian War of 1866, demonstrating his strategic use of each war to set up the next." },
  { q: "The Austro-Prussian War of 1866 (Seven Weeks' War) decisively ended in the Battle of:", opts: ['Sedan', 'Sadowa (Königgrätz)', 'Jena', 'Waterloo'], ans: 1, exp: "The decisive battle of the Austro-Prussian War was at Sadowa (Königgrätz) on 3 July 1866, where Prussia's superior needle-gun rifles and military planning crushed Austria in just seven weeks. The subsequent Treaty of Prague excluded Austria from German affairs permanently and led to the formation of the North German Confederation under Prussian leadership." },
  { q: "The immediate trigger for the Franco-Prussian War of 1870-71 was Bismarck's manipulation of:", opts: ['A territorial dispute over Alsace-Lorraine', 'The Ems Dispatch — Bismarck edited a telegram from King Wilhelm I to Napoleon III to make it appear insulting to France', 'France\'s refusal to allow Prussia to annex Bavaria', 'Napoleon III\'s demand for Rhine territory as compensation for Prussian expansion'], ans: 1, exp: "Bismarck deliberately edited the Ems Dispatch (July 13, 1870) — a telegram about a diplomatic meeting regarding the Spanish succession — to make it appear that Wilhelm I had insulted the French ambassador. When published, it inflamed French public opinion, causing France to declare war, which united the southern German states with Prussia against the common enemy." },
  { q: "Italy's unification was completed in 1870 when which territory was finally annexed?", opts: ['Venice (Venetia), acquired from Austria', 'Nice and Savoy', 'Rome, when French troops protecting the Pope withdrew due to the Franco-Prussian War', 'Sicily, conquered by Garibaldi'], ans: 2, exp: "Rome was the last major territory incorporated into unified Italy. French troops had protected Pope Pius IX's temporal power, preventing Italian annexation. When France withdrew its troops to fight Prussia in 1870, Italian forces entered Rome, completing unification. Rome became the capital of the Kingdom of Italy in 1871." },
  { q: "Why was Italian unification considered slower and more complicated than German unification?", opts: ['Italy lacked any nationalist leaders of the calibre of Bismarck', 'Italy had no equivalent of the Zollverein to create economic unity first', 'Italy faced the additional obstacle of the Pope\'s temporal power (Papal States), French protection of Rome, Austrian control of the north, and deeper regional economic and cultural divisions', 'The Italian population actively opposed unification and preferred the existing state system'], ans: 2, exp: "Italy faced unique obstacles: Austrian military dominance in the north (Lombardy-Venetia), the Pope's temporal Papal States splitting the peninsula, French protection of Rome, and vast cultural-economic divisions between the industrialising north and feudal south. Germany's path, while complex, lacked the religious-geopolitical obstacle of a powerful Pope with international backing." },
  { q: "The term 'the sick man of Europe' in the 19th century referred to:", opts: ['Austria-Hungary, weakened by internal nationalist revolts', 'Russia, weakened by the Crimean War defeat', 'The Ottoman Empire, whose territories were being contested by rising Balkan nationalist states', 'Spain, which had lost its colonies'], ans: 2, exp: "The Ottoman Empire was called 'the sick man of Europe' from the 1850s onward because it was in terminal decline — losing territories to Balkan nationalist revolts (Greece 1821-29, Serbia, Bulgaria, Romania) and great-power pressure. This decline created a 'Eastern Question' that European powers competed to exploit, making the Balkans highly volatile." },
  { q: "Greece's struggle for independence (1821-29) was significant for European nationalism because:", opts: ['It was the first successful nationalist uprising that resulted in a fully democratic republic', 'It was the first successful revolt against an empire that created a new nation-state, inspiring later nationalists across Europe', 'It showed that nationalism could only succeed with military assistance from Britain', 'Greece\'s success demonstrated that large multi-ethnic empires were inherently stable'], ans: 1, exp: "Greek independence (1829, recognised at London Conference) was the first successful 19th-century nationalist liberation movement, dismembering the Ottoman Empire and creating a new nation-state. European Romantic poets like Byron supported it. It proved that nationalist uprisings could succeed, inspiring Italian, German, Polish, and Balkan nationalists." },
  { q: "Pan-Slavism, as championed by Russia in the Balkans, was viewed with suspicion by Austria-Hungary primarily because:", opts: ['Russia was using Pan-Slavism to spread Orthodox Christianity into Catholic areas', 'Pan-Slavism threatened to break up the multi-ethnic Austro-Hungarian Empire, which contained millions of Slavic peoples (Czechs, Slovaks, Poles, Croats, Slovenes, Serbs)', 'Austria-Hungary was itself a Slavic empire that wanted exclusive control of the Pan-Slavic movement', 'Pan-Slavism advocated the destruction of all European monarchies'], ans: 1, exp: "Austria-Hungary contained numerous Slavic ethnic groups — Czechs, Slovaks, Poles, Slovenes, Croats, and some Serbs. A Pan-Slavic movement led by Russia threatened to inspire these groups to demand autonomy or independence, potentially dissolving the Habsburg Empire. The 1908 annexation of Bosnia and the 1914 assassination of Franz Ferdinand by a Pan-Slavic nationalist reflected these tensions." },
  { q: "When comparing German and Italian unification, which statement is MOST accurate about the role of Realpolitik?", opts: ['Realpolitik was used by Mazzini in Italy but not by Bismarck in Germany', 'Bismarck used Realpolitik in Germany through calculated wars; Cavour used a similar pragmatic diplomatic approach in Italy, allying with France against Austria', 'Both Germany and Italy relied primarily on popular democratic revolutions rather than top-down elite statecraft', 'Realpolitik succeeded in Italy but failed in Germany, where idealism was more effective'], ans: 1, exp: "Both German and Italian unification ultimately succeeded through top-down elite statecraft rather than popular revolution. Bismarck used three calculated wars; Cavour used the Franco-Austrian War (1859) after allying with Napoleon III at Plombières (1858). In both cases, the failures of 1848's liberal-nationalist uprisings showed that idealism alone could not achieve unification without military and diplomatic power." },
  { q: "The role of print culture (newspapers, pamphlets, novels) in 19th-century nationalism can BEST be described as:", opts: ['Largely irrelevant since most populations were illiterate', 'Creating \'imagined communities\' by allowing people who never met to share common languages, histories, and identities', 'Useful only for spreading socialist ideas rather than nationalist ones', 'Primarily serving government propaganda for existing monarchies'], ans: 1, exp: "Benedict Anderson's concept of 'imagined communities' argues that print capitalism — the mass production of texts in common vernacular languages — enabled millions of readers to feel part of the same community without personally knowing each other. Nationalist newspapers, historical novels (like Walter Scott in Scotland), and folk poetry collections were crucial tools for building national consciousness." },
  { q: "Which of the following correctly describes the social composition of 19th-century European nationalist movements and their key limitation?", opts: ['Nationalism was driven by the urban working class, whose interest in democratic rights gave it mass popular support', 'Nationalism was primarily a movement of the educated middle class (lawyers, teachers, students, merchants) who often failed to mobilise peasants and workers around a purely nationalist programme', 'Nationalism united all classes seamlessly because its appeal was universal', 'Nationalism was led by the aristocracy who saw national states as a way to maintain their privileges'], ans: 1, exp: "19th-century nationalist movements were predominantly led by the educated bourgeoisie — lawyers, professors, students, journalists. Their abstract demands for constitutions and national unity often failed to resonate with illiterate peasants worried about land reform or urban workers demanding economic change. This class gap was a key reason 1848 revolutions fragmented and failed." },
  { q: "The 'Peoples of Spring' revolutions of 1848 failed across Europe, but which ONE lasting outcome did they produce?", opts: ['The immediate unification of both Germany and Italy', 'They proved that popular revolution was permanently impossible, leading to only top-down reform', 'They abolished serfdom in the Austrian Empire permanently, even as other revolutionary demands were rolled back', 'They established universal male suffrage across Western Europe'], ans: 2, exp: "While the Austrian government crushed the 1848 revolts politically, Emperor Franz Joseph abolished serfdom permanently (1848) — a concession to peasant demands that could not be reversed. In France, the Second Republic was short-lived but introduced universal male suffrage. These social changes outlasted the political failures of 1848." },
  { q: "The 'July Revolution' of 1830 in France had what impact on European nationalism?", opts: ['It led to the permanent establishment of a French Republic', 'It triggered sympathetic nationalist uprisings in Poland, Italy, and Belgium, demonstrating the international contagion of revolutionary nationalism', 'It caused the Concert of Europe to permanently collapse', 'It directly led to the unification of Germany'], ans: 1, exp: "The July Revolution (1830) toppled Charles X and inspired immediate uprisings in Belgium (which won independence from the Netherlands), Poland (which was crushed by Russia), and parts of Italy (suppressed by Austria). It demonstrated that revolutions were contagious across borders and that the nationalist spirit could not be fully contained by the Vienna system." },
  { q: "Historians distinguish between 'ethnic nationalism' and 'civic nationalism'. Which statement BEST contrasts these in the 19th-century European context?", opts: ['Ethnic nationalism defined the nation by shared culture, language, and ancestry; civic nationalism (as in post-1789 France) defined the nation as a community of citizens sharing political rights regardless of ethnic origin', 'Ethnic nationalism was practised only in Germany; civic nationalism only in France', 'Both types were identical in practice since both excluded minorities from full citizenship', 'Civic nationalism was more common in Eastern Europe; ethnic nationalism in Western Europe'], ans: 0, exp: "Post-1789 French nationalism was civic — any person on French territory who accepted the laws was French regardless of language or ethnicity. German nationalism under Herder and later Bismarck tended toward ethnic-linguistic nationalism — Germanness was defined by culture and language. This distinction had profound implications: civic nationalism was more inclusive, ethnic nationalism more exclusionary." },
  { q: "How did the experience of Napoleon's conquests paradoxically PROMOTE nationalism in the territories he dominated?", opts: ['Napoleon deliberately created nationalist sentiment to make conquered peoples loyal to France', 'Napoleon imposed French laws, administrative uniformity, and the idea of the nation, but his foreign domination provoked resistance that was itself expressed as nationalist opposition', 'Napoleon\'s military defeats in Russia and Spain showed people that nationalism was stronger than empire', 'Napoleon introduced newspapers in vernacular languages, directly creating nationalist print culture'], ans: 1, exp: "Napoleon spread Revolutionary ideals (equality before law, abolition of feudalism) across Europe, introducing the concept of the nation-state. But his imperialism and exploitation aroused nationalist resistance — in Spain (Peninsular War), in German states, in Italy. Nationalism thus had a double parentage in Napoleonic Europe: both inspired by and formed in opposition to French imperialism." },
  { q: "The Polish uprisings of 1830-31 and 1863 against Russian rule failed primarily because:", opts: ['Poland had no nationalist leaders or organisation', 'The Polish uprisings relied solely on diplomatic protests without military action', 'Russia\'s military power was overwhelming, and the great powers — especially Austria and Prussia — refused to support Polish independence as they all had territorial interests in partitioned Poland', 'The Polish population was divided between wanting Russian rule and Austrian rule'], ans: 2, exp: "Poland had been partitioned (1772, 1793, 1795) among Russia, Prussia, and Austria. All three partitioning powers had self-interested reasons to maintain Polish division. Russia crushed the 1830 and 1863 uprisings militarily. Britain and France verbally sympathised but took no action. Polish nationalism became a tragic symbol of nationalism's limits when facing great-power consensus against self-determination." },
  { q: "The Frankfurt Parliament's debate over 'Greater Germany' (Großdeutschland) vs 'Lesser Germany' (Kleindeutschland) centred on:", opts: ['Whether Germany should include Prussia or be dominated by southern German states', 'Whether German unification should include Austria (Greater Germany) or exclude it, unifying only non-Austrian German states under Prussian leadership (Lesser Germany)', 'How large Germany\'s colonial empire should be compared to Britain and France', 'Whether German unification should be led by the working class or the middle class'], ans: 1, exp: "The Frankfurt Parliament debated two paths: Großdeutschland would include Austria (and its large non-German Catholic populations), while Kleindeutschland would exclude Austria and unify under Protestant Prussia. The Austrian Empire's refusal to exclude its non-German territories made Großdeutschland impractical. Bismarck's solution was Kleindeutschland, achieved through the defeat of Austria in 1866." },
  { q: "Which statement BEST explains why the 1848 German revolutionaries at Frankfurt failed to resolve the Schleswig-Holstein question?", opts: ['The Parliament agreed immediately to leave Schleswig-Holstein under Danish rule', 'The Parliament voted to annex the duchies but Prussia then made a separate armistice with Denmark (Malmö, 1848) without consulting the Parliament, exposing the Parliament\'s lack of real power', 'Schleswig-Holstein chose to remain with Denmark through a referendum', 'The duchies were awarded to Austria as compensation for its losses elsewhere'], ans: 1, exp: "When Prussia, responding to nationalist pressure, briefly fought Denmark over Schleswig-Holstein, the Frankfurt Parliament cheered. But Prussia then negotiated the Armistice of Malmö (August 1848) with Denmark independently, without Parliament's consent. The Parliament ultimately had to accept this armistice it had initially rejected, revealing it had no independent military or diplomatic power — a critical exposure of its impotence." },
  { q: "In the context of 19th-century cultural nationalism, the Brothers Grimm's collection of German folk tales served which function?", opts: ['Providing entertainment for German urban working classes', 'Documenting German folk traditions to forge a sense of shared cultural heritage and common German identity rooted in a mythologised peasant past', 'Promoting German imperial expansion in Eastern Europe', 'Arguing for the superiority of German culture over French Enlightenment rationalism'], ans: 1, exp: "Jacob and Wilhelm Grimm published their Kinder- und Hausmärchen (1812-22), collecting German folk tales. This project was not merely literary — it was a nationalist cultural project asserting that a common German folk soul existed through shared oral traditions. Like the Kalevala in Finland, it used culture to construct a national identity independent of political fragmentation." },
  { q: "The concept of 'nations without history' (geschichtslose Völker) was used by some German nationalists to argue:", opts: ['That small nations like Czechs and Slovaks lacked a distinct cultural identity and should be absorbed into larger nations', 'That Germany had no historical claim to territories in Eastern Europe', 'That nations created after 1848 lacked historical legitimacy', 'That Poland had forfeited its right to nationhood by failing in its 1830 uprising'], ans: 0, exp: "Friedrich Engels and some German nationalists argued that 'nations without history' — small peoples like Czechs, Slovenes, or Croats who lacked a medieval statehood tradition — had no right to independent states and would be absorbed into larger nations (German or Hungarian). This argument was used to dismiss the claims of Slavic peoples within the Habsburg Empire." },
  { q: "The Revolutions of 1848 had a particular class dimension in that the liberal middle class turned against the revolution when:", opts: ['Conservative armies became clearly superior in military strength', 'Working class demands went beyond liberal constitutionalism to include social and economic rights, threatening property owners', 'The Catholic Church excommunicated the revolutionary leaders', 'France withdrew its diplomatic support for the European revolutions'], ans: 1, exp: "The 1848 revolutions fractured along class lines. In France, the June Days uprising saw Parisian workers demanding social republic (employment rights, economic equality) suppressed by the bourgeois republic with military force. In Germany and Austria, middle-class liberals feared working-class radicalism more than aristocratic conservatism — this split doomed the revolutions." },
  { q: "Why is the year 1848 described as both 'the revolution of the intellectuals' and ultimately a failure of liberalism?", opts: ['Intellectuals wrote manifestos but refused to participate in actual political institutions', 'The revolution succeeded in its intellectual goals but failed militarily', 'Liberal intellectuals shaped the agenda of 1848 (constitutions, national unity, civil liberties) but lacked the military force and cross-class unity to impose their vision against conservative armies when reaction set in', 'Only educated people supported nationalism; workers and peasants supported conservatism'], ans: 2, exp: "1848 was driven by liberal intellectuals and students who had theorised nationalism and constitutionalism for decades. But theory met the harsh reality of politics: conservative armies remained loyal, princes regrouped, and the peasant-worker coalition fractured. The 'revolution of the intellectuals' showed liberalism's limits — it could inspire but not compel transformation without military or mass social power." },
  { q: "The Hanover composer Ludwig van Beethoven's Ninth Symphony is sometimes associated with European nationalism because:", opts: ['It was composed as the official anthem of the German Confederation', 'Its final movement\'s setting of Schiller\'s \'Ode to Joy\' expressed universal brotherhood that nationalists appropriated as an anthem of their specific national aspirations', 'Beethoven explicitly dedicated it to German unification under Prussia', 'It was banned by Metternich as nationalist propaganda'], ans: 1, exp: "Beethoven's Ninth Symphony (1824) set Schiller's 'Ode to Joy' which celebrated universal human brotherhood. While not explicitly nationalist, nationalists across Europe appropriated its powerful message. Today the 'Ode to Joy' is the anthem of the European Union — an ironic full circle from its nationalist appropriation to representing a supranational European identity." },
  { q: "Which of the following BEST describes the role of the Catholic Church in Italian nationalism?", opts: ['The Church actively supported Italian unification throughout the process', 'The Church was a major obstacle: the Pope opposed Italian unification (which threatened his Papal States), excommunicated King Victor Emmanuel, and declared the Italian state illegitimate until 1929', 'The Church was neutral and played no political role', 'The Church supported Garibaldi\'s republican vision but opposed Cavour\'s monarchism'], ans: 1, exp: "Pope Pius IX, initially reform-minded, became a fierce opponent of Italian nationalism after 1848. He condemned liberalism and nationalism in the Syllabus of Errors (1864), and when Italian troops took Rome in 1870, he declared himself a 'prisoner in the Vatican.' The 'Roman Question' was only resolved by Mussolini's Lateran Treaty (1929). Anticlericalism became a feature of Italian nationalism." },
  { q: "The alliance at Plombières (1858) between Cavour and Napoleon III of France agreed that:", opts: ['France would help Prussia defeat Austria in exchange for the Rhine territories', 'France would support Piedmont-Sardinia militarily against Austria in exchange for Nice and Savoy', 'Napoleon III would personally lead French troops into Rome to protect the Pope', 'France would recognise Italian unification in exchange for a defensive alliance against Prussia'], ans: 1, exp: "At Plombières (July 1858), Cavour secretly met Napoleon III. France agreed to support Piedmont-Sardinia militarily if Austria attacked first (Cavour would provoke Austria) in exchange for Nice and Savoy being ceded to France. This led to the Franco-Austrian War (1859), which drove Austria from Lombardy and set Italian unification in motion." },
  { q: "The term 'Risorgimento' in the context of Italian nationalism means:", opts: ['The Red Shirts movement of Garibaldi', 'The Italian customs union modelled on the Zollverein', '\'Resurgence\' or \'Revival\' — the movement and period of Italian national revival and unification', 'The Republican constitution proposed at the Frankfurt Parliament'], ans: 2, exp: "Risorgimento (meaning 'resurgence' or 'revival') was the term for the 19th-century Italian nationalist movement aimed at unifying Italy and reviving Italian greatness after centuries of foreign domination and political fragmentation. The newspaper Il Risorgimento, founded by Cavour in 1847, gave the movement its name." },
  { q: "Describing the period after 1815, Eric Hobsbawm wrote that nationalism was 'primarily the programme of the liberal middle class.' Which development in the 1860s-70s challenged this characterisation?", opts: ['Nationalism became associated with conservative forces: Bismarck used it from above to unify Germany under Prussian monarchy without liberal constitutionalism', 'The working class completely abandoned nationalist politics for internationalist socialism', 'Nationalism became irrelevant after 1848\'s failures', 'The middle class abandoned nationalism after the Frankfurt Parliament\'s failure'], ans: 0, exp: "After 1848, nationalism shifted from a liberal middle-class programme to being hijacked by conservative state-builders. Bismarck demonstrated that nationalism could serve conservative, authoritarian ends — he unified Germany without a liberal constitution, using nationalism to rally popular support while maintaining aristocratic power. This 'top-down' conservative nationalism became increasingly dominant after 1870." },
  { q: "The Habsburg Empire's response to the 1848 revolutions, after crushing them, was primarily to:", opts: ['Grant genuine constitutional autonomy to all nationalities in the empire', 'Pursue neo-absolutism — centralized rule and suppression of all nationalist and liberal movements — until forced to grant the Compromise (Ausgleich) of 1867 creating Austria-Hungary', 'Expel all non-German nationalities from Vienna', 'Voluntarily dissolve and allow all nations to form separate states'], ans: 1, exp: "Emperor Franz Joseph (r. 1848-1916) initially pursued neo-absolutism after 1849 — centralized government, suppression of national autonomy. Military defeats (Solferino 1859 to France, Sadowa 1866 to Prussia) forced concessions. The Ausgleich (Compromise) of 1867 created the Dual Monarchy — Austria-Hungary — granting Hungarians equality while other nationalities (Czechs, Croats) received nothing, storing up future grievances." },
  { q: "The concept of 'Europe of Nations' (as opposed to an empire or a universal republic) was primarily associated with which nationalist thinker?", opts: ['Metternich, who wanted a league of sovereign states', 'Giuseppe Mazzini, who envisioned a \'Young Europe\' of free democratic nations cooperating as equals', 'Bismarck, who wanted a German-dominated European order', 'Marx and Engels, who saw nation-states as the necessary stage before communist internationalism'], ans: 1, exp: "Mazzini's vision was of a 'Young Europe' — a fraternity of free democratic nations, each with its own distinct character and mission, cooperating peacefully. Unlike both cosmopolitan universalism and imperial domination, his vision was a Europe of nations. However, his republicanism and democratic idealism were sidelined as unification was achieved by conservative monarchical power." },
  { q: "The historian Benedict Anderson argued that nations are 'imagined communities.' Applied to 19th-century Europe, this means:", opts: ['Nations were fictional inventions with no real cultural basis, created purely by propaganda', 'National identity was constructed through shared cultural practices, especially print media, creating a sense of belonging among people who never personally met — an imagined but psychologically real community', 'Only small nations where everyone knew each other were genuine; large nations were imagined and therefore illegitimate', 'Anderson argued nationalism was a product of imperialism imposed from above'], ans: 1, exp: "Anderson's 'imagined communities' concept argues that nations are socially constructed — members feel they belong to a community despite never meeting most fellow members. This imagining was facilitated by print capitalism (shared newspapers and books in common languages), religious decline creating secular community, and the concept of time as simultaneous historical experience." },
  { q: "Which of the following was a key difference between nationalism in Western Europe (France, Britain) and Eastern/Central Europe (German states, Italian states, Balkans) in the first half of the 19th century?", opts: ['Eastern nationalism was older and better established than Western nationalism', 'Western European nationalism operated in the context of existing nation-states seeking to define national character; Eastern nationalism sought to CREATE new nation-states by unifying fragmented peoples or breaking from empires', 'Eastern nationalism was purely ethnic; Western nationalism was purely economic', 'Western European nationalism was more violent and revolutionary than Eastern nationalism'], ans: 1, exp: "France and Britain were already nation-states by the early 19th century — their nationalism was about defining and defending existing national communities. German and Italian nationalists had to first CREATE the political structures (unified states) that didn't yet exist, making their nationalism more urgently linked to state-building. Balkan nationalists sought independence from empires." },
  { q: "The 'Eastern Question' in 19th-century European diplomacy referred to:", opts: ['Russia\'s expansion into Central Asia and its threat to British India', 'The debate over whether Russia should be admitted to the Concert of Europe', 'How to manage the decline of the Ottoman Empire and the competing interests of great powers and emerging Balkan nationalist states in the resulting power vacuum', 'Whether Eastern European states should adopt constitutions modelled on the French or British example'], ans: 2, exp: "The Eastern Question dominated European diplomacy from the 1820s through WWI: as the Ottoman Empire weakened, who would control its territories? Russia wanted warm-water ports and Pan-Slavic influence; Britain feared Russian dominance; Austria feared Slavic nationalism; France had cultural and economic interests. Each Balkan nationalist uprising (Greece 1821, Bulgaria 1876, Serbia, Romania) reignited these tensions." },
  { q: "The Irish rebellion of 1798 and the subsequent Act of Union (1800) connecting Ireland to Britain demonstrated which aspect of nationalism?", opts: ['That nationalism always succeeds when supported by a majority population', 'That nationalism could be violently suppressed and defeated by colonial power, leading to incorporation rather than independence', 'That nationalist movements always require foreign military support to succeed', 'That economic grievances were irrelevant to Irish nationalism'], ans: 1, exp: "The United Irishmen rebellion of 1798, inspired by French Revolutionary ideals and supported briefly by French forces, was crushed by Britain. The Act of Union (1800) then absorbed Ireland into the United Kingdom, abolishing the Irish Parliament. This showed that nationalist uprisings could be violently suppressed and used as justification for deeper imperial integration — the opposite of national self-determination." },
  { q: "The concept of 'natural borders' (frontières naturelles) as used by French nationalists referred to:", opts: ['Borders defined by rivers and mountain ranges that supposedly corresponded to the \'natural\' extent of the French nation', 'The idea that France should control only its existing territory and not expand', 'Rivers as natural trade routes that should define economic rather than political borders', 'The Rhine border France lost to Germany after 1871 which French nationalists sought to recover'], ans: 0, exp: "French nationalists and Revolutionary governments argued France had 'natural borders': the Rhine to the east, the Alps and Pyrenees to the south, and the ocean to the west and north. This concept was used to justify French expansion to the Rhine (acquiring Belgian lands, left bank of Rhine) — demonstrating how nationalism could serve expansionist territorial ambitions as readily as liberation movements." },
  { q: "Balkan nationalism's most explosive manifestation was the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand in 1914 by Gavrilo Princip, who was affiliated with:", opts: ['Young Bosnia — a Pan-Slavic nationalist group inspired by Serbian nationalism and united South Slav ideology', 'The Russian revolutionary movement', 'A Bulgarian nationalist organisation', 'An Austrian anarchist group opposing the Habsburgs'], ans: 0, exp: "Gavrilo Princip was a Bosnian Serb nationalist affiliated with 'Young Bosnia,' a group inspired by Pan-Slavic and Serbian nationalist ideas seeking to unite South Slavic peoples. The assassination triggered WWI, demonstrating how Balkan nationalism — the most explosive nationalism in Europe — had created an 'arc of crisis' that ultimately destroyed the 19th-century European order." }
];
// CH2_EXTRA
const SOCIAL_CH2_EXTRA = [
  { q: "The Dandi March began on 12 March 1930 from Sabarmati Ashram and covered exactly how many miles to reach Dandi on the Gujarat coast?", opts: ['180 miles in 18 days', '240 miles in 24 days', '300 miles in 30 days', '200 miles in 20 days'], ans: 1, exp: "Gandhi's Salt March covered exactly 240 miles (approximately 390 km) from Sabarmati Ashram to Dandi village on the Gujarat coast, taking 24 days (12 March to 5 April 1930). On 6 April 1930, Gandhi picked up a handful of salt, symbolically breaking the Salt Law and launching the Civil Disobedience Movement." },
  { q: "How many carefully selected followers initially accompanied Gandhi on the Dandi March when it began on 12 March 1930?", opts: ['72 followers', '78 followers', '100 followers', '50 followers'], ans: 1, exp: "Gandhi began the Dandi March with 78 carefully selected satyagrahis from Sabarmati Ashram on 12 March 1930. The march attracted massive attention and crowds gathered along the route; by the time it reached Dandi, hundreds more had joined. The symbolic number 78 connected to the satyagrahis who had returned with Gandhi from South Africa." },
  { q: "Gandhi's specific choice of salt as the target of the Civil Disobedience campaign was strategically significant because:", opts: ['Salt production was India\'s largest industry and employed millions directly', 'The salt tax was the single largest source of British Indian revenue', 'Salt was a commodity used by every Indian regardless of religion, class, or region — making the salt law universally resentable', 'Gandhi personally suffered from a salt deficiency illness'], ans: 2, exp: "The genius of the salt satyagraha was its universal relevance: every Indian — rich or poor, Hindu or Muslim, male or female, urban or rural — consumed salt and was equally subject to the hated salt tax. Unlike land revenue or textile boycotts which had differential impact, the salt law united all classes and communities against British authority." },
  { q: "Alluri Sitarama Raju led an armed tribal uprising in the Agency Tract of present-day Andhra Pradesh during 1922-24. His followers were primarily:", opts: ['Urban industrial workers protesting wage cuts', 'Rampa tribal communities (Kondh/Koya) protesting the Forest Regulations that restricted their traditional shifting cultivation', 'Zamindars protesting British land revenue assessments', 'Congress workers protesting the arrest of Gandhi'], ans: 1, exp: "Alluri Sitarama Raju led the Rampa Rebellion (1922-24) among the Rampa tribal communities (Koya, Kondh) in the Godavari Agency tracts. They protested the Madras Forest Regulations (1882) that banned their traditional 'podu' (shifting cultivation) and forest access, as well as their forced recruitment for road construction. Raju combined armed resistance with a claim to spiritual powers, making him a legendary figure." },
  { q: "What was the eventual fate of Alluri Sitarama Raju and how did this affect the tribal rebellion?", opts: ['He was arrested, tried, and sentenced to transportation to the Andaman Islands', 'He was captured and publicly shot by British forces in May 1924, ending the rebellion', 'He escaped to Ceylon and continued guerrilla activities from exile', 'He surrendered and signed an agreement with the British, demobilising his followers'], ans: 1, exp: "Alluri Sitarama Raju was captured by British forces and shot without trial on 7 May 1924. His death ended the Rampa Rebellion. He became a legendary martyr figure in Andhra Pradesh and is now celebrated as 'Manyam Veerudu' (Hero of the Jungle). His rebellion demonstrated that Indian nationalism intersected with tribal resistance to colonial forest laws." },
  { q: "Plantation workers in Bengal (specifically Assam tea garden workers) who refused to leave the reserves during 1921 were acting under which legal restriction?", opts: ['The Rowlatt Act of 1919 which restricted their movement', 'The Inland Emigration Act of 1859, which legally bound contract workers to tea estates and prevented them from leaving without their employer\'s permission', 'The Criminal Tribes Act which classified them as habitual criminals', 'The Sedition Act which restricted political assembly'], ans: 1, exp: "The Inland Emigration Act (1859) bound Assam tea plantation workers to their estates under contract — they could not leave without the employer's consent. In 1921, inspired by Non-Cooperation Movement ideas of swaraj (which they interpreted as freedom to return home), thousands abandoned the tea gardens and tried to return to their villages, defying the law. They were caught and brutally beaten by police." },
  { q: "The Awadh peasant movement of 1920-21, led by Baba Ramchandra, connected to the Non-Cooperation Movement but had distinctly different demands. What were its primary objectives?", opts: ['Reduction of land revenue and abolition of begar (forced labour)', 'Complete abolition of zamindari system and redistribution of land', 'Expulsion of all British planters from Awadh', 'Demanding that Congress recognise Hindi as India\'s national language'], ans: 0, exp: "The Awadh peasant movement under Baba Ramchandra (who had been to Fiji as an indentured labourer) focused on reducing oppressive rents, abolishing begar (unpaid forced labour owed to landlords), and providing social boycott against landlords who mistreated tenants. While connected to national Congress politics, these were local agrarian demands that sometimes alarmed Congress's landowning supporters." },
  { q: "How did the Awadh peasant movement under Baba Ramchandra differ ideologically from Congress's mainstream Non-Cooperation programme?", opts: ['Baba Ramchandra supported British rule while Congress opposed it', 'The peasant movement invoked the religious language of Ramayana and ram-raj, and targeted Indian landlords (talukdars) and moneylenders — going beyond anti-British nationalism to internal class conflict within Indian society', 'Baba Ramchandra opposed the use of non-violence, unlike Gandhi', 'The peasant movement focused only on Punjab, not Awadh'], ans: 1, exp: "Baba Ramchandra organised peasants through Ramayan recitations and the language of ram-raj (Ram's just rule), mobilising religious sentiment. The targets included Indian zamindars and moneylenders, not just the British — creating class tension within the nationalist coalition. Congress leaders like Nehru, who visited Awadh, tried to redirect this energy toward anti-British nationalism, with mixed success." },
  { q: "The Mappila Uprising of 1921 in the Malabar region of Kerala began as an anti-landlord/anti-colonial movement but became communally violent because:", opts: ['British troops used Hindu soldiers to suppress the Muslim Mappilas, provoking religious retaliation', 'The Mappilas were predominantly Muslim tenant farmers who converted some Hindus forcibly, leading the Congress to withdraw support and allowing British forces to use communal framing to suppress it', 'The Hindu Mahasabha organised armed resistance against the Mappila peasants', 'Gandhi personally intervened and declared the Mappilas\' actions criminal'], ans: 1, exp: "The Mappila (Moplah) Rebellion (August-December 1921) began as Mappila Muslim peasant revolt against Hindu landlords and British authority, connected to the Khilafat movement. It became communally violent — with some temples desecrated and forced conversions reported. This gave British authorities the opportunity to frame it as communal disorder, alienating Hindu opinion and weakening the Khilafat-Congress alliance." },
  { q: "Dr. B.R. Ambedkar's fundamental critique of Gandhi and the Congress Party regarding lower-caste Hindus was:", opts: ['That Congress was not sufficiently opposed to British rule', 'That Gandhi\'s concept of varnashrama dharma legitimised caste hierarchy, and Congress representing upper-caste interests could not genuinely represent untouchables, who needed separate political representation', 'That Gandhi\'s non-violent methods were too slow to achieve independence', 'That Congress neglected economic development in favour of political nationalism'], ans: 1, exp: "Ambedkar argued that Gandhi's reform approach — trying to purify Hinduism from within — accepted the caste system's religious basis (varnashrama dharma). Ambedkar insisted untouchability was inseparable from Hinduism and that Dalits needed separate political identity. Their confrontation at the Round Table Conferences and over the Communal Award (1932) and Poona Pact (1932) was a defining conflict in Indian nationalist politics." },
  { q: "The Poona Pact of September 1932 was signed between Gandhi and Ambedkar as a result of:", opts: ['A compromise over the Congress\'s demand for complete independence', 'Gandhi\'s fast-unto-death opposing the Communal Award that had granted Dalits separate electorates — the Pact replaced separate electorates with reserved seats within a joint electorate', 'A dispute over Muslim representation in the Indian legislature', 'British government pressure on Congress to include untouchables in the independence movement'], ans: 1, exp: "The British Communal Award (August 1932) granted untouchables separate electorates (allowing them to elect their own representatives independently). Gandhi fasted against this, arguing it would permanently divide Hindu society. Ambedkar reluctantly signed the Poona Pact (26 September 1932), which replaced separate electorates with reserved constituencies within a joint electorate — but significantly increased the reserved seats from 71 to 148." },
  { q: "The Nagpur Session of the Indian National Congress (December 1920) was significant because:", opts: ['It declared Purna Swaraj (complete independence) as the goal', 'It adopted Gandhi\'s Non-Cooperation Programme and reorganised Congress along linguistic lines with Provincial Congress Committees', 'It was the first Congress session attended by women representatives', 'It expelled Bal Gangadhar Tilak\'s faction from Congress'], ans: 1, exp: "The Nagpur Session (December 1920) was transformative: Congress officially adopted Gandhi's Non-Cooperation Programme (the first time Congress endorsed direct confrontational mass action), reorganised Congress Provincial Committees along linguistic rather than administrative boundaries (preparing for linguistic states), set membership fees at 4 annas to mass-democratise Congress, and replaced the old constitution with Gandhi's new one." },
  { q: "The Lahore Session of the Indian National Congress (December 1929), presided over by Jawaharlal Nehru, passed the historic resolution declaring:", opts: ['Support for Gandhi\'s Salt March to protest the salt tax', 'Purna Swaraj (Complete Independence) as Congress\'s goal, rejecting Dominion Status — and 26 January was designated as Independence (Purna Swaraj) Day', 'The Non-Cooperation Movement against British goods', 'The Congress-Muslim League alliance (Lucknow Pact)'], ans: 1, exp: "The Lahore Session (December 1929) under Nehru's presidency passed the Purna Swaraj resolution, formally rejecting the Dominion Status offered by Viceroy Irwin. The tricolour was unfurled on 31 December 1929. January 26 was declared Independence Day, observed annually with pledges — which is why India chose 26 January 1950 for its Republic Day." },
  { q: "The Karachi Session of the Indian National Congress (March 1931) was significant because it:", opts: ['Condemned Gandhi-Irwin Pact as a betrayal of independence', 'Adopted the Fundamental Rights and Economic Policy resolution, outlining Congress\'s vision of a free India including labour rights, universal suffrage, and state ownership of key industries', 'Elected Subhas Chandra Bose as Congress President for the first time', 'Declared the resumption of the Civil Disobedience Movement'], ans: 1, exp: "The Karachi Session (March 1931), presided over by Vallabhbhai Patel, endorsed the Gandhi-Irwin Pact AND adopted the historic Fundamental Rights and Economic Policy resolution drafted by Nehru. This resolution promised civil liberties, universal adult suffrage, free compulsory primary education, equal wages for men and women, and protection of labour — showing Congress's increasingly progressive social vision." },
  { q: "The key distinction between Moderates and Extremists (Hard Liners) within the Indian National Congress in the early 20th century centred on their method. Which statement is MOST accurate?", opts: ['Moderates supported violence; Extremists opposed it', 'Moderates sought to achieve reforms through petitions, prayers, and constitutional methods; Extremists like Tilak advocated direct mass action, self-reliance (swadeshi), and passive resistance to compel British concessions', 'Moderates were older; Extremists were younger — it was purely a generational divide', 'Moderates wanted dominion status; Extremists wanted a republic, not independence from Britain'], ans: 1, exp: "The Moderate-Extremist split (culminating at Surat 1907) was methodological: Moderates (Gokhale, Naoroji, Mehta) believed in loyal petition, gradual reforms through cooperation with British institutions. Extremists (Tilak, Lajpat Rai, Bipin Chandra Pal — Lal-Bal-Pal) advocated swadeshi (self-reliance), boycott of British goods, national education, and passive resistance — seeing the British as inherently hostile to Indian interests." },
  { q: "Why did the Indian National Congress split at the Surat Session of 1907, and what were the immediate consequences?", opts: ['The Congress split over Gandhi\'s Non-Cooperation Programme which the Moderates opposed', 'The split occurred over the nomination of Rash Behari Ghosh as president (Extremists wanted Tilak) and over the demand to extend the Bengal Partition boycott nationally — British policy of \'Divide and Rule\' successfully fragmented Congress for nearly a decade', 'The Extremists left Congress to form the Muslim League', 'The split was over whether to accept the Morley-Minto Reforms of 1909'], ans: 1, exp: "At Surat (1907), the Extremists disrupted the session when the Moderates nominated their candidate (Ghosh) over Tilak. Physical violence broke out, and the two factions split. Britain exploited this by prosecuting Tilak for sedition (1908), crippling the Extremist wing. Congress remained dominated by Moderates until Tilak's return and the Lucknow Pact (1916) reunited both factions." },
  { q: "Sarojini Naidu's role in the Civil Disobedience Movement was particularly significant because:", opts: ['She was the first woman to lead Congress\'s military wing', 'She led the raid on the Dharasana Salt Works in May 1930 after Gandhi\'s arrest, and her role in the Independence movement made her a symbol of women\'s political leadership', 'She negotiated the Gandhi-Irwin Pact on Gandhi\'s behalf', 'She organised the Indian National Army women\'s regiment during WWII'], ans: 1, exp: "After Gandhi's arrest (5 May 1930), Sarojini Naidu and Abbas Tyabji led the Dharasana Salt Works raid (May 1930). Nonviolent marchers walked into police lines and were brutally beaten without retaliation — an event documented by journalist Webb Miller whose account shocked the world. Naidu had earlier led the Indian Women's delegation to the Round Table Conference. She became President of Congress (1925) — the first Indian woman to do so." },
  { q: "Women's participation in the Civil Disobedience Movement differed from their earlier involvement in the Non-Cooperation Movement (1920-22) in which significant way?", opts: ['Women were completely absent from Non-Cooperation but central to Civil Disobedience', 'In Non-Cooperation, women primarily supported men through spinning and boycott activities within the domestic sphere; in Civil Disobedience, they moved into public spaces — leading marches, picketing liquor/foreign cloth shops, and being arrested in large numbers', 'Non-Cooperation specifically excluded women, while Civil Disobedience included them through a separate Women\'s Congress', 'Women\'s roles were identical in both movements as Gandhi consistently promoted women\'s leadership'], ans: 1, exp: "During Non-Cooperation (1920-22), women's roles largely extended domestic duties — spinning khadi, boycotting foreign goods at home. The Salt Satyagraha radically changed this: women marched, led raids on salt depots, picketed toddy shops and foreign cloth stores in public, and were jailed alongside men. This was a genuine transformation in women's public political participation, though Gandhi himself sometimes confined women to 'appropriate' spheres." },
  { q: "The Gandhi-Irwin Pact of March 1931 included which specific concession regarding the Civil Disobedience Movement?", opts: ['The British agreed to grant India Dominion Status by 1935', 'The Civil Disobedience Movement was to be suspended; the British agreed to release political prisoners (not convicted of violence), permit peaceful picketing, and allow coastal salt-making by villagers — in exchange for Congress participation in the Second Round Table Conference', 'The British agreed to abolish the salt tax permanently', 'The British agreed to hold general elections for a new Indian legislature immediately'], ans: 1, exp: "The Gandhi-Irwin Pact (5 March 1931) was a negotiated compromise: Congress would suspend Civil Disobedience; attend the Second Round Table Conference as the sole representative of Indians; government would release non-violent political prisoners; restore confiscated properties not yet sold; allow peaceful picketing of liquor shops; and permit those living near the coast to make salt for domestic use." },
  { q: "Non-Cooperation was suspended by Gandhi on 12 February 1922 following the Chauri Chaura incident. What specifically happened at Chauri Chaura?", opts: ['Police fired on protesters, killing 23 people, and Gandhi suspended the movement fearing retaliation', 'A crowd of protesters at Chauri Chaura (Gorakhpur district, UP) burned a police station, killing approximately 22 policemen trapped inside — Gandhi suspended the movement as it had turned violent', 'British troops massacred 500 protesters, leading Gandhi to suspend the movement to prevent further loss of life', 'Congress leaders at Chauri Chaura defied Gandhi\'s non-violence directive, causing him to suspend the movement'], ans: 1, exp: "On 5 February 1922, at Chauri Chaura in Gorakhpur district (UP), a procession that had clashed with police set fire to the local police station, burning alive approximately 22 policemen. Gandhi was horrified — this violence contradicted the moral basis of satyagraha. He unilaterally suspended the Non-Cooperation Movement on 12 February 1922, to the dismay of many Congress leaders including Motilal Nehru and C.R. Das." },
  { q: "Gandhi's decision to suspend Non-Cooperation after Chauri Chaura was deeply controversial. What was the main criticism levelled at his decision by senior Congress leaders?", opts: ['The movement should have continued since the Chauri Chaura incident was provoked by police brutality', 'The suspension was premature and betrayed millions who had sacrificed for the movement — the nationalist cause was gaining momentum and one incident should not derail it', 'Gandhi should have escalated to armed resistance after Chauri Chaura', 'The movement should have been redirected to constitutional channels rather than suspended'], ans: 1, exp: "Leaders like Motilal Nehru, C.R. Das, Subhas Chandra Bose, and others felt the suspension was a catastrophic strategic error. The movement was at its peak; thousands were in jail making sacrifices; the government was under serious pressure. Suspending for one incident (they argued) vindicated British claims that Indians were not ready for self-rule and demoralised the mass movement." },
  { q: "Muhammad Ali Jinnah's Two-Nation Theory, which became the ideological basis for the demand for Pakistan, argued:", opts: ['That Muslims formed a separate nation based on religion and distinct culture that could not be adequately protected within a Hindu-majority democratic India', 'That India should be divided into four separate linguistic nations', 'That Muslims should have complete economic autonomy within a federated India', 'That Islam required a separate state in every Muslim-majority country in the world'], ans: 0, exp: "Jinnah articulated the Two-Nation Theory systematically in his presidential address to the Muslim League (Lahore, 1940): Hindus and Muslims were not merely two religious communities but two distinct nations with different histories, cultures, civilisations, legal systems, and social values — and that they could not coexist under a single democratic state where Muslims would always be a permanent minority." },
  { q: "The Lahore Resolution (1940) of the Muslim League, often called the 'Pakistan Resolution', demanded:", opts: ['Full independence from Britain immediately, ahead of Congress', 'A federation of Muslim-majority provinces in northwest and east India as \'Independent States\' — the basis for the eventual demand for Pakistan', 'Separate Muslim electorates in all Indian provinces', 'Partition of India into Hindu and Muslim halves along the Indus River'], ans: 1, exp: "The Lahore Resolution (23 March 1940, Pakistan Day) was moved by A.K. Fazlul Haq and adopted under Jinnah's presidency. It demanded that Muslim-majority areas in northwest India (Punjab, Sind, NWFP, Baluchistan) and northeast India (Bengal) be constituted as 'Independent States' — though the word 'Pakistan' was not used. This became the founding charter of the Pakistan demand." },
  { q: "The Communal Award of 1932 (Ramsay MacDonald's Award) granted which specific representation to different communities that made Gandhi fast unto death in opposition?", opts: ['It gave Muslims twice the representation they had under the Morley-Minto Reforms', 'It granted untouchables (Scheduled Castes) separate electorates — allowing them to elect their own representatives in a first election and then join a joint electorate — to which Gandhi objected as permanently dividing Hindu society', 'It divided Bengal into Hindu and Muslim administrative zones', 'It gave Christians and Anglo-Indians separate legislative chambers'], ans: 1, exp: "The Communal Award (16 August 1932) extended separate electorates to untouchables/Scheduled Castes — they would vote in a preliminary election among themselves, then a general election. Gandhi argued this would permanently divide Hindu society and perpetuate untouchability. He began a fast-unto-death from Yeravda Jail (20 September 1932) that pressured Ambedkar into the Poona Pact." },
  { q: "The Non-Cooperation Movement of 1920-22 was the FIRST mass nationalist movement in India's history. What made it qualitatively different from earlier Congress campaigns?", opts: ['It was the first movement to include any demands about economic issues', 'It was the first time Congress officially demanded independence from Britain', 'For the first time, the nationalist movement moved beyond elite-educated circles to include peasants, artisans, workers, women, and tribal communities through a common programme of non-cooperation with colonial institutions', 'It was the first movement to have any Muslim participation'], ans: 2, exp: "Previous Congress campaigns (1885-1919) were elite-dominated — petitions and memoranda by lawyers and professionals. Non-Cooperation (1920-22) transformed Congress into a mass organisation: students left government schools, lawyers withdrew from courts, council elections were boycotted, foreign cloth was burned, and millions from diverse social backgrounds participated. Gandhi's genius was creating a programme accessible to all, not just the literate elite." },
  { q: "The Rowlatt Act of 1919, which triggered Gandhi's first major all-India campaign, gave the government which specific powers that made it so deeply resented?", opts: ['It imposed a new tax on cotton textiles to fund WWI reparations', 'It allowed the government to imprison political suspects without trial for up to two years and try political cases without juries — extending wartime emergency powers into peacetime', 'It disbanded the Indian National Congress as an illegal organisation', 'It required all Indians to carry identity cards issued by the colonial government'], ans: 1, exp: "The Rowlatt Act (March 1919) extended the wartime Defence of India Act's repressive powers — indefinite detention without trial, trials without juries for 'revolutionary' crimes. Indians who had supported Britain in WWI hoping for political reforms found the reward was more repression. Gandhi called it the 'Black Act' and organised nationwide hartals (work stoppages) against it, drawing millions for the first time." },
  { q: "The Jallianwala Bagh massacre of 13 April 1919 had a transformative psychological impact on Indian nationalism because:", opts: ['It was the first time the British had used military force against Indian civilians', 'General Dyer\'s deliberate, prolonged shooting of an unarmed crowd (379 officially killed, thousands wounded) without warning and the subsequent glorification of Dyer in Britain shattered Indian faith in British justice and fairness', 'It directly caused the Non-Cooperation Movement to begin the following week', 'The massacre killed several senior Congress leaders who were replaced by radicals'], ans: 1, exp: "Dyer ordered troops to fire on an unarmed gathering at Jallianwala Bagh (Amritsar) for 10 minutes (1650 rounds fired), killing 379 officially (estimates much higher), wounding 1200+. When the Hunter Committee mildly censured Dyer, British conservatives raised £26,000 for him — revealing that many in Britain supported such brutality. For Tagore (who returned his knighthood) and millions of Indians, this destroyed confidence in British claims of a 'civilising mission.'" },
  { q: "Simon Commission (1927) was universally boycotted by Indian political parties. The specific reason for the boycott was:", opts: ['The Commission wanted to abolish the Indian civil service', 'The Commission was appointed to recommend constitutional reforms but had no Indian members — all seven members were British MPs', 'Simon was personally disliked by Gandhi for his role in the Rowlatt Act', 'The Commission was tasked with partitioning India, which all parties opposed'], ans: 1, exp: "The Simon Commission (appointed 1927, arrived India 1928) was set up to review the Montagu-Chelmsford Reforms but contained only British members — no Indians. This was a calculated insult: Indians were to have no say in deciding their own constitutional future. Congress, Muslim League, Hindu Mahasabha, and liberals united in 'Simon Go Back' demonstrations. Lala Lajpat Rai was fatally injured when police lathi-charged protesters in Lahore." },
  { q: "The Nehru Report of 1928, drafted by Motilal Nehru's committee, was significant because it:", opts: ['First articulated the demand for complete independence (Purna Swaraj)', 'Proposed India\'s first draft constitution based on dominion status with fundamental rights — but its rejection of separate electorates for Muslims widened the Congress-Muslim League divide', 'Called for immediate partition of India into Hindu and Muslim states', 'Proposed a federation of Indian princely states under British paramountcy'], ans: 1, exp: "The Nehru Report (1928) was drafted to show Indians could devise their own constitution. It proposed dominion status (which Subhas Bose and Jawaharlal Nehru opposed, wanting full independence), fundamental rights, and adult suffrage — but crucially, it rejected separate electorates for Muslims (offering reserved seats instead). Jinnah's 'Fourteen Points' (1929) were a direct response, deepening the Hindu-Muslim political divide." },
  { q: "The concept of 'satyagraha' as practised in India differed from simple passive resistance in what crucial philosophical way?", opts: ['Satyagraha required the satyagrahi to harm opponents to demonstrate moral courage', 'Satyagraha was not merely passive acceptance of wrong but active resistance to injustice through soul-force (truth-force) — the satyagrahi willingly accepted suffering to appeal to the opponent\'s conscience and ultimately convert them', 'Satyagraha was effective only against democratic governments, not colonial ones', 'Satyagraha meant that Indians would never engage in any form of political negotiation with the British'], ans: 1, exp: "Gandhi distinguished satyagraha (truth-force/soul-force) from passive resistance: passive resistance was a weapon of the weak; satyagraha was a weapon of the morally strong who fearlessly accepted suffering to demonstrate the justice of their cause. The satyagrahi's suffering was meant to shame and transform (not merely inconvenience) the oppressor. This philosophical distinction made satyagraha a moral as well as political weapon." },
  { q: "The 'Swadeshi Movement' of 1905-07, triggered by the partition of Bengal, is significant in Indian nationalist history because:", opts: ['It was the first movement to include a demand for complete independence from Britain', 'It pioneered the methods (boycott of British goods, promotion of Indian industry, national education, physical training) that Gandhi later scaled up in the Non-Cooperation Movement', 'It was the first movement with active Muslim participation at the mass level', 'It directly resulted in the annulment of the partition of Bengal in 1905'], ans: 1, exp: "The Swadeshi Movement (1905-11) was a laboratory for nationalist methods: boycott of British-manufactured goods, promotion of swadeshi (Indian-made) products, samiti (volunteer organisations), national schools, and cultural expression through song and drama. These methods — and their limitations — directly informed Gandhi's later mass movements. Bengal partition was annulled in 1911, giving the movement a symbolic victory." },
  { q: "Dr. B.R. Ambedkar established the Scheduled Castes Federation in 1942. This organisation's relationship with the Congress was best described as:", opts: ['An allied wing of Congress focused exclusively on Dalit economic issues', 'A rival political organisation that challenged Congress\'s claim to represent all Indians, especially untouchables, arguing that caste Hindus could not be trusted to protect Dalit interests in independent India', 'A temporary grouping that merged with Congress in 1945', 'An organisation that supported the Muslim League\'s Two-Nation Theory as a model for Dalit autonomy'], ans: 1, exp: "Ambedkar's organisations (Bahishkrit Hitakarini Sabha 1924, All India Scheduled Castes Federation 1942) were explicitly created as alternatives to Congress, which Ambedkar charged with being dominated by upper-caste Hindu interests. He demanded constitutional safeguards for untouchables that Congress would not voluntarily provide. His critique — that political independence without social revolution would leave untouchables under 'Hindu Raj' — remains a powerful historical argument." },
  { q: "The term 'khilafat' in the Khilafat Movement (1919-24) referred to:", opts: ['A campaign for Muslim political representation in the Indian legislature', 'The defence of the Ottoman Caliphate — the institution of the Khalifa (Caliph), considered the spiritual and temporal head of Islam, which Britain threatened to abolish after WWI', 'A demand for Muslim majority provinces in northwest India', 'The campaign against the Rowlatt Act specifically targeting Muslim-majority areas'], ans: 1, exp: "The Khilafat Movement was launched by the Ali Brothers (Muhammad Ali and Shaukat Ali) to defend the Ottoman Sultan-Caliph, whom Muslims worldwide regarded as the head of the Islamic world (Khalifa). After WWI, Britain's treatment of the defeated Ottoman Empire threatened to abolish the Caliphate. Gandhi saw the Khilafat issue as an opportunity for Hindu-Muslim unity against the British." },
  { q: "The decision to link the Khilafat and Non-Cooperation movements was Gandhi's strategic masterstroke but ultimately had negative long-term consequences because:", opts: ['It alienated Hindu businessmen who relied on Ottoman trade', 'It allowed the Muslim League to take control of Congress', 'When the Khilafat cause collapsed after Turkish nationalists under Kemal Atatürk abolished the Caliphate (1924), the religious-political basis of Hindu-Muslim unity was removed, and the communal politics of the mid-1920s filled the vacuum', 'The British successfully portrayed the alliance as a Muslim conspiracy against Hinduism'], ans: 2, exp: "The Khilafat-Non-Cooperation alliance was a tactical success (1920-22) creating unprecedented Hindu-Muslim unity, but built on the fragile foundation of a transient religious cause. When Mustafa Kemal Atatürk abolished the Ottoman Sultanate (1922) and Caliphate (1924) as part of Turkish modernisation, the Khilafat cause vanished. The 1920s then saw rising communal violence (Malabar 1921, Kohat 1924), as the religious basis of the alliance had never addressed deeper political tensions." },
  { q: "The Indian National Army (INA/Azad Hind Fauj) of Subhas Chandra Bose differed from Gandhi's approach to independence in what fundamental way?", opts: ['Bose wanted dominion status, not complete independence', 'Bose allied with Japan and Germany to militarily defeat Britain — using armed force as the means to independence, directly rejecting Gandhi\'s non-violent satyagraha', 'Bose wanted independence for only Hindu-majority India, excluding Muslim-majority provinces', 'Bose believed independence could only come through the United Nations, not through military action'], ans: 1, exp: "Bose (Netaji) believed that British power could only be removed by military force, not moral persuasion. He established the Azad Hind Government (1943) in Singapore, reorganised the Indian National Army from Indian POWs and overseas Indians, and allied with Japan in the 'Imphal Campaign' (1944) to liberate India militarily. The INA trials (1945) made Bose's soldiers national heroes regardless of the military failure." },
  { q: "The Cripps Mission of 1942 failed because of the Congress's rejection of its main terms. What was Congress's primary objection?", opts: ['Cripps offered complete independence immediately, which Congress considered premature', 'The Mission offered only post-war dominion status (not immediate independence) and crucially allowed provinces to opt out of the Indian Union — which Congress saw as setting up the partition of India', 'The Mission refused to include any Indians in the War Cabinet', 'Cripps insisted on Muslim League\'s prior agreement before offering any terms to Congress'], ans: 1, exp: "The Cripps Mission (March-April 1942) offered a Constituent Assembly after the war to draft a constitution giving India dominion status — but provinces and princely states could opt out, creating de facto Pakistan. Gandhi called it 'a post-dated cheque on a crashing bank.' Congress rejected the 'opt-out' clause as partition; the Muslim League rejected the offer as insufficient. The failure led directly to the Quit India Movement (August 1942)." },
  { q: "The Quit India Movement of August 1942 was distinctive because:", opts: ['It was the most carefully planned and disciplined of Gandhi\'s movements, with clearly defined objectives', 'Gandhi\'s \'Do or Die\' call triggered a massive spontaneous uprising when the entire Congress leadership was arrested within hours of the Bombay resolution — workers, peasants, and students took over without central direction', 'The Movement succeeded in forcing the British to begin immediate transfer of power', 'The Muslim League and Communist Party both supported the Quit India Movement'], ans: 1, exp: "The August Revolution's distinctive feature was its spontaneous, leaderless character. When the British arrested the entire Congress Working Committee within hours of the Bombay resolution (8 August 1942), the movement had no central command. Workers, students, and peasants took matters into their own hands — attacking telegraph lines, railways, and government buildings. Parallel governments were established in some areas. It was suppressed brutally within weeks but showed Britain could not hold India indefinitely." },
  { q: "The partition of Bengal in 1905 by Lord Curzon was justified officially as an administrative measure, but Indian nationalists argued its real purpose was:", opts: ['To reduce the administrative burden on the Bengal Lieutenant-Governor', 'To divide and weaken the politically conscious Bengali middle class by separating Hindu-majority western Bengal from Muslim-majority eastern Bengal — deliberately using religion to undercut nationalist unity', 'To create a more efficient judicial system by separating rural from urban districts', 'To facilitate economic investment in eastern Bengal\'s jute industry'], ans: 1, exp: "Curzon claimed the partition (October 1905) was purely administrative (Bengal was too large). Nationalists, led by Tilak, Aurobindo Ghose, and Bipin Chandra Pal, argued it deliberately divided Hindus and Muslims — making western Bengal (Hindu) and eastern Bengal + Assam (Muslim-majority) separate provinces to weaken the Bengali nationalist movement that was a centre of anti-colonial activity. The resulting Swadeshi Movement eventually forced annulment in 1911." },
  { q: "Bal Gangadhar Tilak's use of Ganapati festivals (from 1893) and Shivaji Jayanti celebrations was historically significant for Indian nationalism because:", opts: ['He used these to promote Hindu-Muslim unity', 'He transformed private religious observances into public political events, creating a shared platform for nationalist mobilisation outside colonial controls — reaching masses who could not attend political meetings', 'He was trying to revive Maratha imperial ambitions under British rule', 'These celebrations were specifically designed to train revolutionary volunteers for armed struggle'], ans: 1, exp: "Tilak (1856-1920) reorganised the Ganapati festival (from 1893) as a public event bringing thousands together — using the religious occasion to deliver nationalist speeches, promote swadeshi, and build community solidarity. Similarly, the Shivaji Jayanti celebrated the Maratha king as a nationalist hero. This use of popular religion to mobilise masses was a precursor to Gandhi's technique of connecting nationalism to familiar cultural forms." },
  { q: "The Indian National Congress's position on communal representation underwent a significant shift between the Lucknow Pact (1916) and the Nehru Report (1928). What was this shift?", opts: ['Congress moved from opposing separate electorates to accepting them, then back to opposing them', 'Congress moved from opposing to accepting reservation of Muslim seats (Lucknow Pact 1916 accepted separate electorates for Muslims), then the Nehru Report (1928) rejected separate electorates — this reversal deepened mistrust between Congress and the Muslim League', 'The Muslim League consistently supported the Nehru Report\'s position throughout this period', 'Congress consistently opposed all forms of communal representation from 1916 through independence'], ans: 1, exp: "The Lucknow Pact (1916) saw Congress formally accept separate Muslim electorates (a concession to bring the League into the national movement). But the Nehru Report (1928) reversed this — proposing joint electorates with reserved seats. Jinnah, who had worked within Congress, saw this as a betrayal and responded with his Fourteen Points. This reversal is a crucial inflection point in Congress-League relations and the path to partition." },
  { q: "The Deoband school of Islamic thought in India and the Aligarh movement of Sir Syed Ahmed Khan differed in their approach to Indian Muslims' relationship with British power. Which statement best captures this?", opts: ['Deoband collaborated with British power; Aligarh opposed it', 'Both Deoband and Aligarh agreed that Muslims should boycott British education but differed on whether to use Persian or Arabic as the language of instruction', 'Deoband emphasised Islamic education and in the late 19th century generally distanced from British rule; Aligarh (Muslim Anglo-Oriental College, 1875) actively promoted Western/English education to equip Muslims for government service in British India', 'Deoband was a political party; Aligarh was purely a religious reform movement'], ans: 2, exp: "Sir Syed Ahmed Khan established Aligarh (1875) to reconcile Islam with modern science and English education, arguing Muslims must engage with British power to survive. Deoband (founded 1867) focused on traditional Islamic scholarship and in the early phase was associated with the 'Deoband fatwa' supporting the 1857 uprising. These different orientations created two intellectual traditions that shaped Muslim political thought in colonial India." },
  { q: "The Provincial Elections of 1937 (under the Government of India Act 1935) had a significant impact on Congress-Muslim League relations. The main political consequence was:", opts: ['The Muslim League won majorities in all Muslim-majority provinces, confirming its status as the sole representative of Indian Muslims', 'Congress formed governments in 8 of 11 provinces but refused to form coalition governments with the Muslim League in UP and elsewhere, causing Jinnah to conclude that Hindus and Muslims could not politically coexist under Congress-dominated democracy', 'Congress and the Muslim League formed a grand coalition government at the Centre', 'The elections confirmed that religion was irrelevant to Indian electoral politics'], ans: 1, exp: "The 1937 elections showed Congress's massive popularity but the Muslim League's relative weakness (it won only 109 of 482 Muslim seats). When Congress formed governments alone in UP — refusing a coalition that would have given the League ministers — Jinnah argued this proved that under a democratic India, Muslims would be permanently subordinate to Congress (read: Hindu) rule. This experience radicalised Jinnah toward the Two-Nation Theory and the Pakistan demand." },
  { q: "The 'August Offer' of 1940 by Viceroy Linlithwaite was rejected by Congress because:", opts: ['It granted India immediate dominion status, which Congress considered humiliating rather than independence', 'It offered only expansion of the Viceroy\'s Executive Council and post-war dominion status — not independence — while guaranteeing that no future constitutional arrangement would be imposed without the consent of minorities (effectively a Muslim League veto)', 'It required Congress to support Britain in WWII unconditionally', 'It excluded provincial governments from any role in war policy decisions'], ans: 1, exp: "The August Offer (8 August 1940) came during Churchill's premiership, when Britain desperately needed Indian support for WWII. It offered expanded Executive Council representation, post-war Constituent Assembly, and crucially — no future constitution against the 'consent of large minorities.' Congress rejected both the meagre offer and the minority veto (which it saw as allowing the Muslim League to block independence). The League cautiously welcomed the minority guarantee." },
  { q: "What was the significance of the Indian National Congress founding the Hindustani Prachar Sabha and promoting Hindustani as a national language, and how did this create controversy?", opts: ['It successfully united all linguistic communities around Hindustani as the national language by 1935', 'The promotion of Hindustani (a blended Hindi-Urdu) as the national language was intended to bridge Hindu and Muslim communities, but it alienated South Indian (Tamil, Telugu) and Bengali speakers who feared northern-language dominance — contributing to later language rights demands', 'Hindustani was chosen because it was spoken by more Indians than any other single language', 'The controversy was entirely between Hindi and Urdu speakers and had no significance for South India'], ans: 1, exp: "Congress promoted Hindustani (which combined Hindi and Urdu elements, accessible to both Hindu and Muslim North Indians) as the common language. Gandhi championed this. However, Tamil, Telugu, Kannada, and Bengali speakers resisted any policy that might make North Indian languages dominant over their own. This language controversy — a form of cultural nationalism within nationalism — foreshadowed the post-independence linguistic states reorganisation debates." },
  { q: "How did tribal communities' participation in nationalist movements differ from that of the urban educated class in terms of their specific grievances?", opts: ['Tribal communities\' grievances were identical to urban Indian nationalists and they were absorbed seamlessly into the mainstream movement', 'Tribal communities joined nationalist movements primarily because of specific local grievances — loss of forest rights, land alienation, forced labour, and money-lender exploitation — rather than abstract ideals of nationalism, creating movements that intersected with but were not identical to mainstream Congress nationalism', 'Tribal communities were entirely absent from the nationalist movement until after 1942', 'Tribal communities\' grievances were exclusively economic with no connection to colonial rule'], ans: 1, exp: "Tribal participation in nationalist struggles (Rampa Rebellion 1922-24, Tana Bhagat movement in Jharkhand, Birsa Munda's Ulgulan 1899-1900) was driven by specific grievances about colonial forest laws, land alienation to moneylenders, and forced labour — not abstract nationalism. Gandhi tried to incorporate tribal grievances into the nationalist framework, but the disconnect between tribal local concerns and the national-constitutional programme often created tensions." },
  { q: "The concept of 'swaraj' as understood by different social groups in colonial India was NOT uniform. Gandhi's swaraj, peasant swaraj, and working-class swaraj referred to different things. Which statement BEST illustrates this multi-layered meaning?", opts: ['Swaraj uniformly meant independence from Britain for all groups', 'Gandhi\'s swaraj meant individual self-rule through moral discipline; peasants interpreted it as freedom from zamindari and colonial revenue; workers as freedom from factory exploitation — creating a coalition with divergent meanings that held together only against the common enemy of British rule', 'Swaraj meant independence for the educated class and land redistribution for the poor — Congress resolved this by adopting the socialist economic programme of the working class', 'Only Gandhi had a concept of swaraj; other groups used the term \'independence\' to describe their political goal'], ans: 1, exp: "The word 'swaraj' was powerfully polysemous: Gandhi's concept was spiritual (self-mastery) and political (self-governance). But Awadh peasants heard it as freedom from zamindars; Assam tea workers as freedom to leave plantations; tribals as freedom to use forests. This ambiguity was nationalists' greatest strength (uniting diverse grievances) and potential weakness (different groups would want different things from free India)." },
  { q: "The Indian National Congress's dilemma regarding the caste system can be described as:", opts: ['Congress consistently championed Dalit rights against Hindu orthodoxy throughout the independence movement', 'Congress needed both Dalit voters and upper-caste leaders, creating a tension between social reform and maintaining political unity — often leading to rhetorical commitment to caste abolition without structural challenges to caste privilege within the organisation', 'Congress officially endorsed the varna system as the natural social order until 1945', 'There was no tension as Congress was internally democratic and representative of all castes equally'], ans: 1, exp: "Congress's caste contradiction was structural: most of its leadership and funding came from upper-caste urban professionals and landowning rural elites who benefited from caste hierarchy. Genuinely transforming caste would alienate these core supporters. Gandhi's 'Harijan' campaigns were sincere but addressed untouchability through Hindu reform rather than structural dismantling. Ambedkar's critique — that Congress's social conservatism was incompatible with genuine equality — had deep historical validity." }
];
// CH3_EXTRA
const SOCIAL_CH3_EXTRA = [
  { q: "The Brundtland Report (1987), formally titled 'Our Common Future', defined sustainable development as:", opts: ['Development that meets the needs of developing countries without constraining the development of industrialised nations', 'Development that uses only renewable natural resources and completely avoids use of non-renewable resources', 'Development that meets the needs of the present without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs', 'Development that is environmentally sustainable only if it also produces economic growth above 5% annually'], ans: 2, exp: "The Brundtland Commission (World Commission on Environment and Development) in 'Our Common Future' (1987) defined sustainable development as 'development that meets the needs of the present without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs.' This definition has two key concepts: 'needs' (especially of the world's poor) and 'limitations' imposed by technology and social organisation on the environment's ability to meet present and future needs." },
  { q: "In the resource classification framework used in CBSE Class 10 Geography, what is the precise difference between a 'stock' and a 'reserve'?", opts: ['Stock and reserve are synonyms — both refer to quantities of a resource available for immediate use', 'A stock is a resource where technology to extract it exists but it is uneconomical to do so; a reserve is a portion of stock that can be extracted profitably with current technology', 'A stock refers to resources that have been surveyed; a reserve refers to unsurveyed resources', 'Stock = non-renewable resources; reserve = renewable resources held back from current use'], ans: 1, exp: "In resource classification: Potential resources are known but not yet fully utilised; Actual/Developed resources are surveyed with quality and quantity determined; Reserves are a subset of actual resources that can be extracted with existing technology at acceptable cost and are awaiting use; Stock is a resource where technology to extract it doesn't yet exist (water in hydrogen form — we lack economic hydrogen extraction). Reserve ⊂ Actual ⊂ Total resources." },
  { q: "The distinction between 'potential resources' and 'actual/developed resources' in resource classification is best illustrated by which Indian example?", opts: ['Gold deposits in Karnataka (actual) vs crop yield from irrigated Punjab (potential)', 'The solar energy potential of Rajasthan (potential — surveyed but not fully developed) vs coal in Jharkhand (actual — surveyed, developed, and in use)', 'Forest reserves in Assam (potential) vs fish in the Exclusive Economic Zone (actual)', 'Underground water in the Ganges basin (potential) vs petroleum in Bombay High (actual)'], ans: 1, exp: "Potential resources are those present in a region but not yet fully utilised due to lack of technology or economic feasibility. Rajasthan has enormous solar energy potential (highest solar insolation in India) but much of it is undeveloped — making it a potential resource. Jharkhand's coal is surveyed, quantified, and actively mined — an actual/developed resource. This distinction drives resource planning decisions." },
  { q: "Resource planning in India involves a three-stage process. Which statement CORRECTLY describes all three stages in order?", opts: ['Identification → Evaluation → Exploitation', 'Identification and inventory of resources across regions → Evolving a planning structure with appropriate technology/skills → Matching resource development plans with overall national development plans', 'Survey → Allocation → Conservation', 'Assessment of national needs → Regional survey → International comparison'], ans: 1, exp: "Resource planning has three stages: (1) Identification and inventory — surveying, mapping, qualitative and quantitative estimation of resources; (2) Evolving a planning structure — with appropriate technology, skills, and institutional setup; (3) Matching resource development plans with national development plans, ensuring technology, finance, and institutional support align with the development plan's goals. This process requires expertise across disciplines including geology, economics, and social science." },
  { q: "Garrett Hardin's 'Tragedy of the Commons' (1968) describes a situation where shared resources are overexploited. Which Indian example BEST illustrates this concept?", opts: ['The nationalisation of coal mines in 1973', 'Overgrazing of village commons (wasteland/pastures) in Rajasthan, overfishing of coastal waters beyond the Exclusive Economic Zone, and depletion of groundwater from shared aquifers in Punjab/Haryana', 'Government forest reserves being converted to agricultural land', 'Private mining companies depleting iron ore in Goa'], ans: 1, exp: "Hardin's Tragedy of the Commons occurs when rational self-interest leads each individual to overuse shared resources, eventually destroying them for everyone. Indian examples include: overgrazing of common pastures in Rajasthan; overfishing in coastal waters (unregulated artisanal fishing); and excessive groundwater extraction from shared aquifers in Punjab/Haryana/Rajasthan — each farmer pumps more since the aquifer is shared, collectively depleting it for all." },
  { q: "Biopiracy, in the context of resource use, refers to:", opts: ['The theft of biological specimens from protected national parks', 'The use of military force by powerful nations to control oil reserves of developing countries', 'The appropriation by multinational corporations or individuals of traditional biological knowledge and genetic resources of developing nations without fair compensation, often through patents', 'The smuggling of endangered species across international borders'], ans: 2, exp: "Biopiracy involves using traditional knowledge about biological resources (medicinal plants, crop varieties, etc.) originating from local communities in developing countries, patenting products derived from this knowledge in developed countries, and then selling those products back — without compensating the knowledge-holders. Famous cases include patents on neem (antifungal), turmeric (wound healing), and basmati rice. India has fought several biopiracy cases successfully at international IP bodies." },
  { q: "The Indian state with the highest percentage of forest cover relative to its total geographical area is:", opts: ['Uttarakhand', 'Madhya Pradesh', 'Mizoram', 'Arunachal Pradesh'], ans: 2, exp: "Mizoram has the highest percentage of forest cover relative to its total geographical area (approximately 85-90%), being a densely forested northeastern state. While Madhya Pradesh has the largest absolute area under forests in India, Mizoram and other northeastern states have the highest forest cover as a proportion of their geographical area. This distinction between absolute area and proportional coverage is important for understanding regional forest distribution." },
  { q: "Land degradation through waterlogging, primarily affecting Punjab, Haryana, and western Uttar Pradesh, occurs specifically because:", opts: ['Excessive rainfall beyond 2000mm annually saturates the soil', 'Over-irrigation (especially by canal irrigation without adequate drainage) raises the water table, bringing salts to the surface and making soil saline and unfit for cultivation', 'Deforestation on hill slopes sends excess water to plains, flooding fields', 'Industrial effluent disposal in irrigation canals contamination the soil'], ans: 1, exp: "Waterlogging in the irrigated plains of Punjab, Haryana, and western UP occurs when canal irrigation supplies more water than crops need and drainage infrastructure is inadequate. The water table rises; capillary action brings dissolved salts (naturally occurring) to the surface as water evaporates — leaving salt crusts (soil salinisation). This 'white alkali land' becomes uncultivable. India has approximately 2 million hectares of waterlogged and 3.7 million hectares of salt-affected land." },
  { q: "Wind erosion, the dominant form of land degradation in Rajasthan, western Haryana, and southern Punjab, is most effectively controlled by:", opts: ['Terracing the land and constructing bunds along contours', 'Planting shelter belts of trees perpendicular to the prevailing wind direction, along with stabilising sand dunes with grasses and controlling grazing', 'Constructing check dams across drainage channels', 'Deep ploughing to bring subsoil moisture to the surface'], ans: 1, exp: "Wind erosion is controlled primarily through windbreaks (shelter belts) — rows of trees planted perpendicular to prevailing winds that reduce wind speed and trap moving soil particles. In Rajasthan, khejri trees (Prosopis cineraria) play this role traditionally. Sand dune stabilisation uses hardy grasses (sewan grass) and shrubs to bind the surface. Overgrazing, which removes this natural cover, is a major driver of desertification in the Thar." },
  { q: "The specific form of land degradation most characteristic of mining areas in Jharkhand, Odisha, and Chhattisgarh is:", opts: ['Salinisation from irrigation-induced waterlogging', 'Sheet erosion from heavy monsoon rainfall on flat terrain', 'Overburden dumps, mine spoil heaps, subsidence, and pollution from mine drainage that render large areas unproductive and destroy local drainage patterns', 'Deforestation-induced gully erosion on hill slopes'], ans: 2, exp: "Mining operations create distinct land degradation: overburden dumps (waste rock from open-cast mining) cover productive land; acidic mine drainage pollutes water; subsidence occurs over underground mines; and topsoil is stripped and not replaced. Jharkhand and Odisha's coal and iron ore mining areas show massive spoil heaps and degraded land. Unlike agricultural degradation, mining degradation requires specific reclamation (revegetation of spoil heaps, neutralising acid drainage)." },
  { q: "Gully erosion, which creates 'ravines' (badlands), is most extensively developed in which physiographic/ecological zone of India?", opts: ['Coastal Maharashtra', 'The Chambal basin (Madhya Pradesh/Rajasthan) and Yamuna basin ravines in Uttar Pradesh, where deeply incised gullies form extensive ravine land', 'The Deccan lava plateau of Maharashtra', 'The Brahmaputra flood plains of Assam'], ans: 1, exp: "Gully erosion and ravine formation are most extensive in the Chambal watershed (MP/Rajasthan) and Yamuna-Ganges doab (UP), covering approximately 3.97 million hectares. Deep gullies (ravines/badlands/beehad) are formed when runoff concentrates and cuts into unconsolidated alluvial soils. These 'chambal ravines' historically were also the territory of dacoits (bandits) who used the difficult terrain for hiding." },
  { q: "The exact percentage of India's total geographical area under forest cover (as per government targets under National Forest Policy 1988) should be:", opts: ['23.28% as currently recorded', '33% of total geographical area (the policy target)', '40% of total geographical area', '17% of total geographical area'], ans: 1, exp: "India's National Forest Policy (1988) set a target of bringing 33% of India's total geographical area under forest and tree cover (compared to the actual coverage of approximately 23-24%). The 33% target is based on the minimum area required to maintain ecological balance, soil and water conservation, and climate regulation. This gap between actual (23%) and target (33%) represents approximately 10% of India's 3.29 million sq km." },
  { q: "Which of the following correctly describes the difference between 'renewable' and 'non-renewable' resources with an example that challenges simplistic definitions?", opts: ['Renewable resources always regenerate faster than they are consumed; non-renewable never regenerate', 'Renewable resources can regenerate within human timescales if not overexploited (fish, forests, soil); non-renewable resources (coal, petroleum) formed over millions of years and cannot regenerate within any practical human timescale — but even \'renewable\' resources like soil and groundwater become effectively non-renewable when depleted faster than they naturally regenerate', 'Renewable = solar and wind; non-renewable = everything else', 'Renewable resources are always found on the surface; non-renewable resources are always underground'], ans: 1, exp: "The renewable/non-renewable distinction is a rate comparison, not an absolute property. Fish stocks are renewable but become locally extinct if overfished (e.g., collapsed North Sea cod fishery). Soil takes 200-1000 years to form 1cm of topsoil — so eroded soil is effectively non-renewable on human timescales. Groundwater in fossil aquifers (like Rajasthan's Thar) has recharge rates measured in millennia. The depletion rate vs. regeneration rate ratio determines practical renewability." },
  { q: "The concept of 'resource curse' or 'paradox of plenty' refers to the empirical observation that:", opts: ['Countries with abundant natural resources tend to use them more efficiently than resource-poor countries', 'Countries with abundant natural resources (oil, minerals) tend to have slower economic growth, higher inequality, and weaker democratic institutions than resource-poor countries — possibly due to corruption, Dutch disease, and conflict over resource rents', 'Abundant natural resources always lead to colonisation by more technologically advanced nations', 'Resource-rich countries always have higher agricultural productivity since they can afford irrigation infrastructure'], ans: 1, exp: "The 'resource curse' (Sachs and Warner, 1995) describes how resource-abundant countries often perform paradoxically poorly economically. 'Dutch disease' occurs when resource export revenues appreciate the exchange rate, making manufacturing uncompetitive. Corrupt elites capture resource rents. Foreign companies and governments compete for access, funding authoritarian regimes. Nigeria (oil), Congo (minerals), and Bolivia (tin historically) are examples. This makes thoughtful resource management a development challenge, not just an environmental one." },
  { q: "Soil as a resource differs from most other natural resources in that it is:", opts: ['Entirely renewable regardless of how it is managed', 'A complex, living ecosystem (containing bacteria, fungi, worms, insects alongside minerals and organic matter) that can be either conserved or destroyed by human activities — making it simultaneously a biotic AND abiotic resource', 'A purely geological resource with no biological components', 'Uniformly distributed across India with minor regional variations'], ans: 1, exp: "Soil is uniquely a bio-abiotic resource: it contains mineral particles (abiotic) alongside living organisms (biotic) — bacteria, fungi, actinomycetes, protozoa, nematodes, earthworms — that cycle nutrients, decompose organic matter, and maintain soil structure. One teaspoon of healthy soil contains more microorganisms than there are humans on Earth. Practices that destroy soil biota (excessive chemical use, compaction, waterlogging) can devastate soil fertility irreversibly." },
  { q: "Laterite soil, found in the high rainfall areas of Western Ghats, Eastern Ghats, and northeast India, is characterised by:", opts: ['High organic matter content, dark colour, and suitability for all crops', 'Intense leaching in high rainfall areas that removes silica and leaves iron and aluminium oxides, making it acidic, red/yellow in colour, and generally low in nitrogen and potassium — suitable mainly for tea, coffee, and cashew', 'High calcium carbonate content making it basic/alkaline and ideal for wheat cultivation', 'Formed from alluvial deposits, deep, and excellent for sugar cane'], ans: 1, exp: "Laterite soils form under high temperature and heavy rainfall conditions through intense chemical weathering and leaching. Heavy rain leaches away silica, lime, and most nutrients, leaving iron and aluminium hydroxides (laterite = Latin later = brick). They are acidic, low in fertility, and hard when dry. However, they support plantation crops adapted to acidic conditions — tea in Assam/Darjeeling, coffee in Western Ghats, cashew nuts in Kerala/Goa." },
  { q: "Regur or Black Cotton Soil (Vertisols) has a unique physical property that makes it both agriculturally valuable and an engineering challenge. This property is:", opts: ['It is exceptionally light and porous, allowing rapid drainage', 'It swells significantly when wet (absorbing water) and shrinks and cracks deeply when dry, creating a self-ploughing effect but also causing problems for buildings and roads built on it', 'It has a natural pH of 9-10, making it ideal for cotton but toxic to other crops', 'It is naturally saturated with calcium carbonate at all depths, preventing any vertical drainage'], ans: 1, exp: "Regur (Black Cotton Soil/Vertisols) has high clay content (particularly montmorillonite clay) that gives it extreme shrink-swell behaviour: when wet, it swells and becomes sticky/waterproof; when dry, it shrinks and develops deep cracks (up to 1.5m). This crack-and-seal cycle aerates soil from below ('self-mulching') reducing irrigation needs. But buildings crack, roads buckle — making these soils engineering nightmares in Maharashtra/MP/Gujarat's Deccan Trap region." },
  { q: "Alluvial soil, India's most widespread and agriculturally productive soil type, is classified into two types based on age. These are:", opts: ['Laterite (old) and Terra Rosa (young)', 'Bhangar (older alluvium, found on older terraces) and Khadar (newer alluvium, found on lower flood plains, deposited by recent floods) — Khadar being more fertile due to fresh deposition', 'Red-yellow alluvium of Peninsular India and grey alluvium of Indo-Gangetic Plains', 'Sandy alluvium (Rajasthan) and clayey alluvium (Bengal)'], ans: 1, exp: "Alluvial soils are divided into Bhangar (old alluvium on older, higher terraces — calcareous, sometimes contains kankar nodules, less fertile) and Khadar (new alluvium on active flood plains — fine, light-coloured, renewed by annual floods, more fertile). Khadar in the Ganges flood plain supports intensive rice-wheat agriculture. The age difference reflects different depositional histories of the river system." },
  { q: "India's national land use statistics categorise land into several categories. Which of the following land use categories is NOT typically included in India's official Land Use Statistics?", opts: ['Forest land', 'Culturable wasteland', 'Fallow lands', 'Ocean floor resources within India\'s EEZ'], ans: 3, exp: "India's official Land Use Statistics (from state revenue records) cover: Forests; Land not available for cultivation (barren land, built-up areas, roads); Other uncultivated land (permanent pastures, miscellaneous tree groves, culturable wasteland); Fallow lands (current and other fallow); and Net Sown Area. Ocean floor resources within the Exclusive Economic Zone (EEZ) are categorised separately under marine resources and are not part of the land use statistics that cover the country's land surface." },
  { q: "The Net Sown Area (NSA) in India is approximately 45.5% of the total land area, but the Gross Cropped Area (GCA) is higher than NSA. This difference occurs because:", opts: ['GCA includes cultivable waste that is counted twice', 'GCA = NSA × cropping intensity; land cultivated more than once in a year is counted multiple times in GCA (e.g., double-cropped land counts once in NSA but twice in GCA)', 'GCA includes forest land used for shifting cultivation', 'GCA counts both irrigated and rain-fed areas separately while NSA only counts irrigated land'], ans: 1, exp: "Net Sown Area (NSA) counts each piece of land once regardless of how many crops it grows per year. Gross Cropped Area (GCA) counts the same land multiple times if multiple crops are grown annually. GCA = NSA × Cropping Intensity. India's cropping intensity is approximately 140-150% (1.4-1.5 crops per land area per year). States with irrigation and year-round water access like Punjab (200%+ CI) have GCA much greater than NSA." },
  { q: "The 'Chipko Movement' of 1973 in Uttarakhand (then UP) is significant for resource conservation because:", opts: ['It was the first environmental movement in India to use non-violent resistance to prevent commercial logging of Himalayan forests — villagers, especially women, hugged trees to prevent felling', 'It was a movement to demand government compensate villagers for floods caused by deforestation', 'It was an agricultural reform movement that used the slogan \'hug the earth\' for soil conservation', 'It was a movement that successfully lobbied the government to create the first national park in the Himalayas'], ans: 0, exp: "The Chipko Movement (1973, Gopeshwar, Chamoli district) began when villagers, led by Chandi Prasad Bhatt and later associated with Sunderlal Bahuguna, hugged trees to prevent logging by a sports goods company. Women especially participated, understanding that deforestation caused landslides, flood damage, and loss of forest produce they depended on. It led to a logging ban in Uttarakhand hills (1980) and inspired global environmental activism." },
  { q: "Global resource inequality refers to the mismatch between which two dimensions of global resource distribution?", opts: ['The mismatch between renewable and non-renewable resource deposits in developed vs. developing countries', 'The concentration of the world\'s resource reserves in developing countries while the economic and technological power to extract, process, and benefit from them resides in developed countries — leading to unequal terms of trade and exploitation', 'The fact that some countries have too many resources while others have too few, requiring international redistribution', 'The unequal distribution of water resources that leaves some nations with permanent water scarcity regardless of economic development'], ans: 1, exp: "Global resource inequality has multiple dimensions: Resource reserves are often concentrated in developing countries (oil in Middle East/Africa, minerals in Africa/South America, biodiversity in tropical countries) while technological and financial capacity to exploit them belongs to developed countries. This creates dependency, unequal terms of trade ('commodity curse'), and patterns of exploitation perpetuating underdevelopment. The CBSE textbook connects this to imperialism and the post-colonial critique of development." },
  { q: "The 'resource planning' approach recommended in the CBSE curriculum recognises that resources alone cannot drive development. Which historical example supports this argument?", opts: ['Japan\'s post-WWII economic miracle despite having very few natural resources — showing technology, human capital, and institutional quality matter more than raw resource endowment', 'India\'s rapid economic growth from its abundant coal and iron ore reserves after independence', 'The Green Revolution, which created agricultural abundance through technology in resource-poor regions', 'Saudi Arabia\'s development model, which shows that resource wealth is always transformable into sustainable development'], ans: 0, exp: "Japan has almost no significant mineral or energy resources yet became the world's second-largest economy by the 1980s through manufacturing, technology, and human capital. Conversely, mineral-rich Congo remains extremely poor. This comparison, used in development economics, supports the textbook's argument that the quality of resource development planning, institutions, and technology matters more than the mere presence of resources." },
  { q: "The specific soil conservation method of 'contour bunding' is most effective for reducing:", opts: ['Wind erosion on flat Rajasthan plains', 'Sheet and rill erosion on gentle to moderate slopes by constructing earthen barriers (bunds) along contour lines that slow and divert runoff before it can erode the slope', 'Waterlogging in canal-irrigated Punjab fields', 'Saline soil conditions in the Rann of Kutch'], ans: 1, exp: "Contour bunding involves constructing earthen embankments along contour lines (lines of equal elevation) on slopes. When rain falls, runoff hits the bund, slows down, and infiltrates into the soil rather than flowing downslope carrying topsoil. This controls sheet erosion (thin uniform removal of topsoil) and rill erosion (small channels forming in slope). It is widely used in the semi-arid Deccan Plateau where slopes are gentle to moderate and rainfall intense." },
  { q: "Terrace cultivation (bench terracing) as a soil conservation measure is primarily used in:", opts: ['Flat alluvial plains of the Ganges to prevent flooding', 'Steep hill slopes of the Himalayas, Western Ghats, and Eastern Ghats to convert slope into flat cultivable terraced steps, drastically reducing run-off velocity and erosion', 'Coastal regions to prevent sea water intrusion', 'Black cotton soil regions of Maharashtra to prevent cracking'], ans: 1, exp: "Terracing converts continuous steep slopes into a series of flat benches (terraces) separated by risers. By eliminating slope gradient (or greatly reducing it), terrace farming prevents rainfall from gaining velocity as it flows downhill — the primary driver of erosion. In the Himalayan region (Uttarakhand, Himachal Pradesh) and Western/Eastern Ghats, terracing has been practiced for centuries and allows cultivation on slopes that would otherwise be entirely unusable." },
  { q: "Strip cropping as a soil conservation practice specifically addresses which erosion mechanism?", opts: ['It prevents aeolian (wind) erosion by using the height differential between tall and short crops to disrupt wind flow', 'It controls water erosion on gentle slopes by alternating strips of dense vegetation (meadow grasses or dense crops) with crop rows, so the dense strips trap soil carried by runoff from the crop rows', 'It prevents waterlogging by allowing alternating dry and wet strips', 'It reduces soil compaction by ensuring alternating strips rest while others are cultivated'], ans: 1, exp: "Strip cropping places alternating strips of grass, legumes, or small grains perpendicular to the slope or prevailing wind. The dense vegetation strips act as sediment traps — water flowing from row crop strips (corn, sorghum) slows when it hits the grass strips, depositing eroded soil. Over time, soil accumulates behind the dense strips, creating natural terraces. This method is especially effective on gentle to moderate slopes in the semi-arid tropics." },
  { q: "The NCERT textbook distinguishes between 'individual ownership' resources and 'community ownership' resources. In India's historical context, which traditional system represented community ownership of natural resources?", opts: ['Zamindar system under Mughal and British periods', 'Gram Sabha-managed village commons (shamlat deh in Punjab, gram Sabha land in UP) — pastures, ponds, woodlands used collectively by the entire village community', 'Government-owned public sector enterprises managing mineral resources', 'Joint family (Hindu undivided family) ownership of agricultural land'], ans: 1, exp: "Traditional India had extensive community-owned natural resources: shared grazing lands (gochar), village ponds, woodlands (raksha), wastelands — managed collectively by the gram sabha (village assembly). British colonial law systematically converted these commons to state property or allowed privatisation, disrupting the traditional governance systems that had prevented their overexploitation. The loss of commons devastated landless and marginally landed communities who depended on them." },
  { q: "Why is it incorrect to describe India as having a 'uniform' distribution of any major soil type?", opts: ['Because India has only one type of soil uniformly distributed across the entire subcontinent', 'Because India\'s geological diversity (ancient Peninsular Deccan, fold mountains, alluvial plains), climate gradients (arid northwest to tropical southwest), and vegetation diversity creates a complex mosaic of soil types — alluvial in the plains, black in Deccan Trap, red-yellow in Peninsular uplands, laterite on humid coasts, mountain soils in Himalayas', 'Because soil surveys have not yet been completed for most of India\'s territory', 'Because India\'s soil types change every season with monsoon flooding'], ans: 1, exp: "India's soil diversity reflects its geological history: the Indo-Gangetic Plain has deep alluvial soils from Himalayan erosion; the Deccan Trap basalt region (Maharashtra/MP/Gujarat) has black regur soils; the Peninsular Shield's ancient crystalline rocks produce red-yellow soils; heavy rainfall zones (Western Ghats, Northeast) produce laterites; Thar Desert has arid brown soils; and Himalayan slopes have shallow mountain soils. This diversity requires regionally specific agricultural and conservation strategies." },
  { q: "The concept of 'carrying capacity' in resource management refers to:", opts: ['The maximum tonnage a ship can carry when transporting resources internationally', 'The maximum population (human or animal) that a given environment can sustainably support without irreversible degradation of its resource base', 'The rate at which an economy can absorb new resource technologies', 'The maximum depth to which open-cast mines can be dug before becoming geologically unstable'], ans: 1, exp: "Carrying capacity is the maximum population that a defined environment can sustainably support given available resources (food, water, space) and waste assimilation capacity — without reducing the environment's ability to support that population in the future. It applies to livestock on grazing land (overgrazing when exceeded), fish in water bodies (overfishing), and humans in a region. Exceeding carrying capacity leads to resource degradation and eventual population crash." },
  { q: "The Rio Earth Summit of 1992 was significant for global resource management because:", opts: ['It established the World Trade Organisation to regulate international resource trade', 'It produced Agenda 21 — a comprehensive blueprint for sustainable development covering environment, development, and social equity — and established the Convention on Biological Diversity and Framework Convention on Climate Change', 'It banned nuclear energy globally and mandated transition to renewables by 2000', 'It established the principle that developing countries must pay developed countries for environmental damage caused during industrialisation'], ans: 1, exp: "The Rio Earth Summit (UN Conference on Environment and Development, June 1992) was the first major global environmental governance milestone. It produced: Agenda 21 (sustainable development action plan); Convention on Biological Diversity (protecting ecosystems); UN Framework Convention on Climate Change (leading to Kyoto Protocol); and the Statement of Forest Principles. It established 'common but differentiated responsibilities' — all nations must act, but developed nations bear greater responsibility for historic pollution." },
  { q: "In the context of India's resource challenges, the 'Green Revolution' of the 1960s-70s exemplifies which tension in resource development?", opts: ['The tension between economic development and food security', 'It increased wheat and rice production dramatically (solving food security) but created long-term resource problems including groundwater depletion, soil salinisation from over-irrigation, soil fertility decline from monoculture, and loss of crop biodiversity', 'The Green Revolution had no negative environmental consequences', 'The tension between public-sector and private-sector investment in agriculture'], ans: 1, exp: "The Green Revolution (HYV seeds + irrigation + fertilisers) doubled grain production but at environmental cost: Punjab/Haryana groundwater dropped from 5m to 30m+ depth due to tube-well irrigation of wheat-rice monocultures; soil salinity and waterlogging spread; traditional crop varieties (landraces) were abandoned losing genetic diversity; excessive fertiliser use reduced natural soil fertility. This tradeoff between short-term production and long-term resource sustainability is central to understanding sustainable development." },
  { q: "The 'common but differentiated responsibilities' principle, established at Rio 1992, is most relevant to which dimension of the global resources debate?", opts: ['Developing countries own most biological resources, so they have primary responsibility for biodiversity conservation', 'Since developed countries industrialised first (causing historical cumulative pollution/resource depletion) and have more financial and technological capacity, they must take greater responsibility for environmental restoration — while developing countries have the right to develop but must do so sustainably', 'All countries have equal responsibility for climate change regardless of their historical emissions', 'Small island states have differentiated (greater) responsibilities for ocean conservation due to their geographical situation'], ans: 1, exp: "'Common but differentiated responsibilities' acknowledges that while all nations share responsibility for global environmental problems, their historical contribution to those problems and their financial/technological capacity to address them differs. The US (industrialised 150 years on fossil fuels) has greater responsibility than India or Bangladesh (currently developing). This principle was India's key argument at climate negotiations — insisting on development space while acknowledging environmental limits." },
  { q: "The specific problem of 'shifting agriculture' (jhum/swidden cultivation) in northeastern India represents which type of resource challenge?", opts: ['It is purely a soil erosion problem with no other ecological dimensions', 'It represents a traditional sustainable system under low population pressure that becomes ecologically unsustainable when population growth reduces the fallow period below the forest\'s regeneration time, converting a sustainable rotation into permanent deforestation', 'Jhum cultivation is illegal in India and has been completely eliminated', 'Shifting agriculture has no connection to forest degradation since it only cultivates grasslands'], ans: 1, exp: "Jhum cultivation (shifting agriculture) traditionally involved clearing forest, cultivating for 2-3 years, then moving to new plots — allowing 15-20 year fallow periods for forest regeneration. Under low population density this was sustainable. But population growth has compressed fallow cycles to 3-5 years — insufficient for full forest recovery. Combined with increasing jhum frequency, this has contributed to deforestation in Nagaland, Mizoram, Meghalaya, and Manipur." },
  { q: "Overgrazing as a cause of land degradation is most severe in which of the following regions, and why?", opts: ['Tamil Nadu delta — because buffaloes and cattle damage paddy field bunds by grazing', 'Rajasthan, semi-arid parts of Maharashtra, Gujarat, and arid zones of MP — where low and erratic rainfall means slow grass recovery, high livestock populations relative to carrying capacity, and the privatisation of commons has concentrated remaining grazing on marginal lands', 'West Bengal — because the high livestock density in an agricultural state exceeds carrying capacity', 'The Himalayan alpine pastures of Uttarakhand and HP — which experience intense summer grazing pressure from migratory herds'], ans: 1, exp: "Overgrazing-induced desertification is most severe in the arid and semi-arid zones — Rajasthan (Thar), parts of Gujarat, Maharashtra's dry Deccan, and southern MP — where: (1) annual rainfall is too low for rapid vegetation recovery; (2) livestock numbers (traditionally a form of wealth/insurance) often exceed the land's carrying capacity; and (3) progressive enclosure of common grazing lands concentrates pressure on remaining areas. The Rajasthan desert is advancing eastward at 0.5km/year partly due to overgrazing." },
  { q: "The National Wastelands Development Board and NRSA (National Remote Sensing Agency) have estimated India's total degraded land to be approximately:", opts: ['5 million hectares', '13-14 million hectares', '130-175 million hectares (varying by methodology)', '400 million hectares — nearly all of India'], ans: 2, exp: "Estimates of India's degraded land vary significantly by definition and methodology: NRSA using remote sensing estimates approximately 130-175 million hectares affected by various forms of degradation (water erosion, wind erosion, waterlogging, salinity, mining, etc.). This is roughly 40-53% of India's total land area of 328 million hectares. The variation in estimates reflects definitional differences — mild degradation vs. severe degradation — and illustrates why resource management data interpretation requires methodological care." },
  { q: "The 'precautionary principle' in environmental management, increasingly incorporated into Indian environmental law (e.g., Environment Protection Act 1986), states:", opts: ['Environmental protection should only be undertaken when economic growth exceeds 8% annually', 'Where there is credible scientific uncertainty about whether an action will cause environmental harm, preventive action should be taken rather than waiting for full scientific proof of harm', 'Industries should self-regulate their environmental impacts without government intervention', 'Economic development should always take precedence when it conflicts with environmental protection'], ans: 1, exp: "The precautionary principle (Rio Declaration, Principle 15) inverts normal legal burden of proof for environmental decisions: instead of requiring proof of harm before restricting an activity, it requires those proposing a potentially harmful activity to prove it is safe. Under this principle, India has stopped use of certain pesticides, restricted coastal construction, and imposed 'polluter pays' principles — though its implementation is inconsistent." },
  { q: "The United Nations Environment Programme (UNEP) was established following the Stockholm Conference of 1972. Its establishment reflected which recognition about global resource management?", opts: ['That individual nations had proven incapable of managing their own resources and needed direct UN supervision', 'That environmental and resource problems transcend national boundaries and require coordinated international governance, monitoring, and technical assistance', 'That multinational corporations were more effective at resource management than governments', 'That nuclear resource use required international oversight to prevent weapons proliferation'], ans: 1, exp: "The Stockholm Conference (1972, 'Only One Earth') established that environmental problems — acid rain, ocean pollution, ozone depletion, species extinction — did not respect national borders and could not be addressed by individual countries acting alone. UNEP was created as the UN system's environmental conscience and coordinating body. It has since produced landmark assessments (Global Environment Outlook) and helped negotiate major treaties (Montreal Protocol, CITES, Basel Convention)." },
  { q: "In the CBSE resource classification system, iron ore mined from Jharkhand (surveyed, quantified, being extracted) is categorised as:", opts: ['Potential resource', 'Stock', 'Reserve', 'Actual/Developed resource'], ans: 3, exp: "Iron ore actively mined in Jharkhand has been surveyed with known quantity and quality, can be extracted with existing technology at current costs, and is currently being used — making it an Actual/Developed resource (the highest level of utilisation in the classification). The sub-categories within this are: Reserve (surveyed, extractable, but not yet extracted) and Actual/Developed (currently being utilised). Since Jharkhand's iron ore is being mined currently, it qualifies as an Actual/Developed resource." },
  { q: "The 'Agenda 21' programme adopted at the Rio Earth Summit (1992) is significant for local resource management because:", opts: ['It transferred all resource management authority from national governments to the United Nations', 'It recognised that sustainable development must be pursued at the local level — through community participation, decentralised governance, and linking environment with livelihood — introducing the concept of Local Agenda 21 for city/village-level sustainability planning', 'It mandated that 21% of all national budgets be spent on environmental protection by 2021', 'It established a global carbon tax system where resource-rich countries compensate resource-poor countries'], ans: 1, exp: "Agenda 21 (with its 40 chapters covering social and economic dimensions, conservation, strengthening major groups, and means of implementation) emphasised that sustainable development required action at the local level — not just international agreements. 'Local Agenda 21' encouraged cities and communities to develop their own sustainability plans. In India, this influenced watershed management programmes, joint forest management, and panchayati raj involvement in local resource management." },
  { q: "Sustainable development requires 'intra-generational equity' in addition to 'inter-generational equity.' The difference between these two concepts is:", opts: ['Inter-generational equity is about sharing resources between generations; intra-generational equity is about sharing resources fairly WITHIN the current generation — meaning that today\'s poor must also have access to resources, not just future generations', 'Both terms refer to the same principle stated in different ways', 'Intra-generational equity refers to equity within individual countries; inter-generational to equity between countries', 'Inter-generational equity was a concept of the Brundtland Report; intra-generational equity was coined at the Rio Summit'], ans: 0, exp: "Brundtland's 1987 definition emphasised both: inter-generational equity (future generations must have access to adequate resources — the 'our future' in 'Our Common Future') and intra-generational equity (the world's current poor must have their needs met, not just future people). A sustainable development path that preserves resources for future generations while allowing current poverty to continue is considered incomplete — hence the emphasis on poverty eradication alongside environmental sustainability." }
];
